{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '478,512p;374,390p;513,530p;1063,1081p;688,722p' docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/council-q/library-pack.md\"","aggregated_output":"## v10: Will China's authorities authorize Chinese AI companies to purchase Nvidia H200 chips by 2027-01-31?\n\n- Looks at: China's authorization of purchases of Nvidia H200 chips by Chinese AI companies\n- Opened 2026-07-08 from: China to let AI firms buy Nvidia H200s, says report (straitstimes.com); due 2027-01-31; open\n- Research groups: China authorization of Nvidia H200 purchases\n- Answer ladder:\n  1. Yes: China's authorities authorize Chinese AI companies to purchase Nvidia H200 chips by 2027-01-31.  <== current\n  2. No: China's authorities have not authorized Chinese AI companies to purchase Nvidia H200 chips by 2027-01-31.\n- History: 28 judgements; first judged 2026-07-08 at rung 1; rung moves: 2026-07-14 1->2, 2026-07-15 2->1, 2026-07-16 1->2, 2026-07-17 2->1, 2026-07-28 1->2, 2026-07-29 2->1\n- Latest judgement 2026-09-20, why changed: The rung is unchanged, but e5070 adds a distinct report that Chinese regulators mostly blocked H200 imports, changing the evidence supporting the forecast. It strengthens the case for restricted access, while not establishing a complete block and therefore not outweighing the evidence for selective authorization.\n- Latest reasoning: The reference sits at rung 2: NVIDIA's Q3 FY2027 guidance assumes no China Data Center compute revenue. That is a sales assumption, not a forecast that Chinese authorities will authorize no purchases. The evidence implies narrow, case-by-case authorization is more likely than no authorization by the deadline: reports describe permission for selected buyers and some deliveries, and a report says official exceptions were issued to named firms. The evidence that China mostly blocked imports and restricted sales under U.S. licenses argues against broad access, but does not outweigh the evidence for selective authorization; the binary outcome therefore remains rung 1.\n- Evidence in force: 98 (party_statement 43, rumour 20, fact 18, third_party_view 17)\n- Evidence cited by the latest judgement:\n  - e135 [rumour; 2026-07-08; push toward_first] Chinese officials reportedly informed Alibaba, ByteDance and DeepSeek that they would be permitted to buy some Nvidia H200 chips; the purchases remain subject to approval. | source: China to let AI firms buy Nvidia H200s, says report (straitstimes.com) input gnews:c8c39f971721fd820aab5c1c\n  - e172 [rumour; 2026-07-09; push toward_first] Information reported that China plans to permit Alibaba, ByteDance, and DeepSeek to buy Nvidia H200 chips; no date was given. | source: The Download: a nuclear landmark, and China eyes Nvidia chips (technologyreview.com) input gnews:50a84fa6b2f3eb33f29948a4\n  - e173 [rumour; 2026-07-09; push toward_first] An unnamed source told the South China Morning Post that the Chinese government plans to let selected companies, including Alibaba, buy limited numbers of Nvidia H200 chips; no date was given. | source: Game-changer? Why China is finally letting its AI firms buy the Nvidia H200 (scmp.com) input gnews:91684b863d274b10246d0c13\n  - e338 [fact; 2026-07-14; push toward_last] Beijing refused to authorize Chinese companies to buy Nvidia H200 GPUs and relied on domestic semiconductor manufacturers to address the shortfall. | source: Nvidia slashes list of authorized customers in Asia in a bid to reduce AI chip smuggling, report claims — company sent field inspectors, called customers to check if business is genuine after pressure from Washington (tomshardware.com) input gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb\n## v14: How much total revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?\n\n- Looks at: NVIDIA FY2027 total revenue\n- Opened 2026-07-10 from: Nvidia’s $1-trillion wipeout leaves AI titan trading at pre-boom prices (latimes.com); due 2027-02-28; open\n- Research groups: none\n- Answer ladder:\n  1. Strongly bullish: Total revenue is at least $450 billion.\n  2. Bullish: Total revenue is at least $400 billion but less than $450 billion.  <== current\n  3. Neutral: Total revenue is at least $350 billion but less than $400 billion.\n  4. Bearish: Total revenue is at least $300 billion but less than $350 billion.\n  5. Strongly bearish: Total revenue is less than $300 billion.\n- History: 71 judgements; first judged 2026-07-10 at rung 3; rung moves: 2026-07-26 3->2, 2026-08-11 2->3, 2026-08-19 3->2\n- Latest judgement 2026-09-20, why changed: The rung is unchanged, but the current assessment incorporates the newly specified estimate of more than $100 billion of demand left unfulfilled next year as a supply-side counterweight. Its timing and revenue impact are not specific enough to reduce the FY2027 run-rate estimate.\n- Latest reasoning: The reference is about $411 billion for FY2027, which sits at rung 2; the cited Zacks growth estimate also implies about $405 billion using FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion. The operating run-rate implies about $419 billion: estimate Q3 at roughly $113.5 billion by applying the cited average 3.1% beat to the $110.16 billion high end of guidance, then carry the guided 12% sequential growth forward to Q4, about $127 billion, alongside reported Q1 and Q2 revenue. Supply constraints are a meaningful downside risk, but the cited estimate of more than $100 billion in unfulfilled demand is for “next year” and is not quantified as a reduction to FY2027 revenue. The implied outcome remains above $400 billion and below $450 billion, so it stays at rung 2.\n- Evidence in force: 1329 (fact 509, party_statement 395, third_party_view 373, rumour 52)\n- Evidence cited by the latest judgement:\n  - e5056 [third_party_view; 2026-09-19; push none] Wall Street analysts expect NVIDIA to generate around $411 billion of revenue in FY2027. | source: We could see the world's first $10 trillion company arrive in less than two years (supercarblondie.com) input gnews:01edb4d1fa96705133b29207\n  - e4702 [third_party_view; 2026-09-14; push none] Zacks consensus estimates NVIDIA FY2027 total revenue growth of 87.4% year over year and EPS growth of 93.5%. | source: NVIDIA vs. Marvell: Which AI Infrastructure Stock Is the Better Buy? (zacks.com) input gnews:1d3bc1ba4c2df5e998884bdb\n  - e4377 [fact; 2026-09-10; push none] NVIDIA reported FY2026 revenue of approximately $215.9 billion, up 65.5% year over year. | source: Advanced Micro Devices vs. Nvidia: Which Semiconductor Stock Is a Better Buy in 2026? (fool.com) input gnews:be51df202b76715f3d9023f3\n  - e238 [fact; 2026-07-12; push toward_first] NVIDIA reported fiscal Q1 2027 total revenue of $81.6 billion, up 85% year over year. | source: Forget GPUs. Nvidia’s Next AI Gold Mine Could Be Even Bigger. (barchart.com) input gnews:82472d26336e1982cf8a3eff\n  - e2497 [fact; 2026-08-26; push toward_first] NVIDIA reported Q2 FY2027 revenue of $96.2 billion, up 18% quarter over quarter and 106% year over year. | source: NVIDIA Announces Financial Results for Second Quarter Fiscal 2027 | NVIDIA Newsroom (NVIDIA Corporation) input nvidianews:nvidia-announces-financial-results-for-second-quarter-fiscal-2027\n  - e2499 [party_statement; 2026-08-26; push toward_first] NVIDIA guided Q3 FY2027 revenue to $108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%, assuming no Data Center compute revenue from China. | source: NVIDIA Announces Financial Results for Second Quarter Fiscal 2027 | NVIDIA Newsroom (NVIDIA Corporation) input nvidianews:nvidia-announces-financial-results-for-second-quarter-fiscal-2027\n  - e3943 [party_statement; 2026-09-04; push toward_first] NVIDIA's FY2027 Q3 guidance implied total revenue growth of 12% sequentially at the midpoint. | source: Jensen Huang's Nvidia Guided for 70% Revenue Growth in Fiscal 2028, Far Above the 44% Wall Street Expected, as Amazon Agreed to Buy 2 Million Nvidia GPUs. Is the Growth Forecast Believable? (fool.com) input gnews:89698c9955cf8aa35b642970\n  - e4761 [third_party_view; 2026-09-15; push toward_first] Manali Pradhan forecast NVIDIA fiscal Q3 2027 revenue of around $113 billion to $114 billion. | source: Prediction: Nvidia's $108 Billion Q3 Guide Will Look Conservative by December (fool.com) input gnews:9fd93b942948c5002e13eb0b\n  - e4762 [third_party_view; 2026-09-15; push toward_first] Manali Pradhan said NVIDIA exceeded the high end of revenue guidance in each of the past 12 quarters, averaging 3.1% above the high end over the past four quarters. | source: Prediction: Nvidia's $108 Billion Q3 Guide Will Look Conservative by December (fool.com) input gnews:9fd93b942948c5002e13eb0b\n  - e5073 [third_party_view; 2026-09-20; push toward_first] NVIDIA's year-over-year revenue growth rate accelerated for four consecutive quarters, from 62% to 73%, 85%, and 106% in Q2 FY2027. | source: Nvidia Stock Hasn't Set a Record in 4 Months. History Says These Stalls Have Ended 2 Ways. (fool.com) input gnews:e5b9c11e2c8ef9754946a897\n  - e4769 [party_statement; 2026-09-15; push toward_last] NVIDIA management expects supply to remain constrained through FY2028 and said higher memory prices are adding pressure. | source: Prediction: Nvidia's $108 Billion Q3 Guide Will Look Conservative by December (fool.com) input gnews:9fd93b942948c5002e13eb0b\n  - e4901 [third_party_view; 2026-09-18; push toward_last] CRN estimated that NVIDIA could leave more than $100 billion of demand for AI infrastructure gear unfulfilled next year because of supply constraints. | source: Analysis: What Nvidia’s Massive Supply-Demand Gap Says About AI Mania (crn.com) input gnews:3a284e233d8b31c62dc66b33\n- A random sample of uncited evidence in force (seed 20261120):\n  - e2817 [party_statement; 2026-08-26] CEO Jensen Huang said non-hyperscaler customers represent about half of NVIDIA's business and are growing 100% annually. | source: Nvidia unleashes 70% growth bombshell and defends against ‘circular financing’ doomsayers (fortune.com) input gnews:4f0b142ac84dc52deff2898a\n  - e2087 [rumour; 2026-08-18] SB Energy is reportedly seeking to secure up to 10 gigawatts of power, mostly from natural gas, for the Ohio facility; no deadline is specified. | source: Nvidia, SB Energy Announce Massive Ohio Data Center for OpenAI (techrepublic.com) input gnews:5d2d15afbe1559d0a5f8d604\n  - e2981 [fact; 2026-08-27] NVIDIA highlighted a local AI initiative optimizing for leading open models, including DeepSeek V4 Flash and Alibaba Qwen 3.8, alongside Google and NVIDIA systems. | source: Nvidia is bolstering support for Chinese open AI models as it warns of White House crackdown (cnbc.com) input gnews:a625887f511b79f425e27824\n  - e5058 [fact; 2026-09-19] NVIDIA reported fiscal Q2 2027 revenue of $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year, including $89 billion of Data Center revenue. | source: Semiconductor Stocks to Buy and Hold Through 2030 (fool.com) input gnews:5feec912090c2af6467eed38\n  - e2737 [party_statement; 2026-08-26] NVIDIA estimated revenue opportunity per gigawatt of data-center capacity at about $18 billion for Hopper, $25 billion for Blackwell, and $40 billion for Vera Rubin, including CPUs, GPUs, networking, systems, and software. | source: NVIDIA Q2 Earnings Call Highlights (marketbeat.com) input gnews:7dfab0010a8ac15786f05387\n\n## v15: How much will NVIDIA's supply commitments reach at the publication of its FY2027 annual report?\n\n- Looks at: NVIDIA supply commitments\n- Opened 2026-07-13 from: Meta Vs. Nvidia: Meta Has Quietly Emerged as the Strongest Mega-Cap Alternative This Summer (247wallst.com); due 2027-02-28; open\n- Research groups: none\n- Answer ladder:\n  1. Strongly bullish: NVIDIA's supply commitments are below $50 billion.\n  2. Bullish: NVIDIA's supply commitments are at least $50 billion and below $100 billion.\n  3. Neutral: NVIDIA's supply commitments are at least $100 billion and below $150 billion.\n  4. Bearish: NVIDIA's supply commitments are at least $150 billion and below $200 billion.\n  5. Strongly bearish: NVIDIA's supply commitments are at least $200 billion.  <== current\n- History: 59 judgements; first judged 2026-07-13 at rung 3; rung moves: 2026-07-16 3->5, 2026-07-23 5->3, 2026-07-27 3->4, 2026-08-01 4->3, 2026-08-06 3->4, 2026-08-22 4->5, 2026-08-26 5->4, 2026-08-27 4->5, 2026-09-01 5->4, 2026-09-05 4->5\n- Latest judgement 2026-09-20, why changed: The rung is unchanged, but the supporting facts and calculation have been updated: the evidence now spells out the FY2028–FY2032 schedule and includes a CEO forecast of doubled 2027 chip sales, alongside further evidence on commitment flexibility and provisions. These refine the replenishment estimate while leaving the implied outcome above $200 billion.\n- Latest reasoning: The disclosed schedule is the reference: $279 billion of commitments, including $92 billion scheduled for the rest of FY2027. At the FY2027 report publication, the schedule alone implies about $187 billion remaining, which is rung 4. I forecast about $215 billion: that $187 billion residual plus an estimated $28 billion of replenishment as NVIDIA secures supply for strong future demand. The CEO's forecast of twice as many chip sales in 2027 and the large recent increase in commitments support continued additions; constrained supply, adjustable agreements, and the small amount scheduled beyond FY2029 temper them. The implied figure remains above the $200 billion rung-5 boundary.\n- Evidence in force: 102 (fact 51, party_statement 29, third_party_view 20, rumour 2)\n- Evidence cited by the latest judgement:\n  - e2612 [fact; 2026-08-26; push toward_last] NVIDIA reported that its supply and capacity commitments rose from $119 billion in the prior quarter to $279 billion as of 2026-07-26. | source: NVDA 10-Q 10-Q (NVIDIA Corporation) input edgar:NVDA:0001045810-26-000075:nvda-20260726.htm\n  - e2851 [fact; 2026-08-26; push toward_first] NVIDIA reported $279 billion in commitments to secure supply and manufacturing capacity, up from $119 billion last quarter, including $92 billion in projected spending for the rest of the fiscal year and $87 billion in FY2028. | source: Amazon just tripled its order of Nvidia chips over ‘surging demand’ (techcrunch.com) input gnews:d949d26fe875780037064b8a\n## v20: Will NVIDIA complete its planned $1 billion investment in NAVER Corp. by 2027-07-24?\n\n- Looks at: Whether NVIDIA completes its planned $1 billion investment in NAVER Corp.\n- Opened 2026-07-24 from: NAVER, NVIDIA and Brookfield to Expand Korea’s National AI Factory Infrastructure Buildout | NVIDIA Newsroom (NVIDIA Corporation); due 2027-07-24; open\n- Research groups: NAVER GAK Sejong AI factory expansion and financing\n- Answer ladder:\n  1. Yes: NVIDIA completes the planned $1 billion investment in NAVER Corp. by 2027-07-24.  <== current\n  2. No: NVIDIA has not completed the planned $1 billion investment in NAVER Corp. by 2027-07-24.\n- History: 46 judgements; first judged 2026-07-24 at rung 1; rung moves: none\n- Latest judgement 2026-09-20, why changed: The rung and its key supporting facts remain unchanged: NVIDIA’s $1 billion investment is planned/agreed, but is conditional on at least $9 billion in committed project financing, and the disclosed Brookfield term sheet is nonbinding. The additional reports largely repeat those facts and do not establish that the financing condition has been met or failed.\n- Latest reasoning: The reference is NVIDIA’s announced plan to invest $1 billion, which sits at rung 1 (Yes). The evidence still makes completion by 2027-07-24 more likely than non-completion: NVIDIA is reported to have agreed to acquire $1 billion in newly issued NAVER shares, and the project is planned to expand, with the announced schedule extending to 2028. The main risk is material: NVIDIA’s investment is conditional on NAVER securing at least $9 billion in committed project financing, while Brookfield’s disclosed term sheet is nonbinding and for up to $9 billion. The joint $10 billion statement does not establish that financing condition or specify NVIDIA’s share. Those facts leave execution uncertain, but do not show the condition has failed or the investment has been cancelled; with the deadline still about a year away, the forecast remains Yes.\n- Evidence in force: 22 (fact 10, party_statement 7, rumour 3, third_party_view 2)\n- Evidence cited by the latest judgement:\n  - e872 [party_statement; 2026-07-24; push toward_first] NVIDIA plans a $1 billion investment in NAVER Corp., subject to customary closing conditions and NAVER securing at least $9 billion in committed project financing. | source: NAVER, NVIDIA and Brookfield to Expand Korea’s National AI Factory Infrastructure Buildout | NVIDIA Newsroom (NVIDIA Corporation) input nvidianews:naver-nvidia-and-brookfield-to-expand-koreas-national-ai-factory-infrastructure-buildout\n  - e873 [fact; 2026-07-24; push toward_last] Brookfield entered into a nonbinding term sheet to fund up to $9 billion for the NAVER AI factory project. | source: NAVER, NVIDIA and Brookfield to Expand Korea’s National AI Factory Infrastructure Buildout | NVIDIA Newsroom (NVIDIA Corporation) input nvidianews:naver-nvidia-and-brookfield-to-expand-koreas-national-ai-factory-infrastructure-buildout\n  - e942 [party_statement; 2026-07-25; push toward_first] Naver founder Lee Hae-jin said Nvidia and Brookfield will jointly invest $10 billion in Naver; the article does not break out Nvidia’s share. | source: Naver secures combined $10 billion investment from Nvidia, Brookfield (koreajoongangdaily.com) input gnews:f95a4ad4a5002dba0bc1b6d4\n  - e945 [party_statement; 2026-07-25; push toward_first] Naver plans to more than triple the size of its South Korean data centre using the funding. | source: Nvidia to invest US$1 billion in Naver and expand SK Group accord (businesstimes.com.sg) input gnews:99a1ba68b933cb3eeb687120\n  - e950 [fact; 2026-07-25; push toward_last] Brookfield agreed to a non-binding term sheet to fund up to US$9 billion for the Naver data-centre project. | source: Nvidia to invest US$1 billion in Naver and expand SK Group accord (businesstimes.com.sg) input gnews:99a1ba68b933cb3eeb687120\n  - e993 [fact; 2026-07-27; push toward_first] NVIDIA agreed to acquire $1 billion in newly issued Naver shares to co-build an AI data center. | source: Nvidia slips 4% as blockbuster AI spending spree triggers balance sheet jitters (investing.com) input gnews:c871b9605dd781113ba40cef\n  - e1002 [fact; 2026-07-27; push toward_first] NVIDIA agreed to invest $1 billion in Naver to support an AI data center being developed with Brookfield; the investment timing is not specified. | source: NVIDIA Pursues $750 Billion in New AI Deals Despite Financing Scrutiny (gurufocus.com) input gnews:e7777109769b68857021b63b\n  - e1054 [fact; 2026-07-27; push toward_first] NAVER, NVIDIA and Brookfield plan to expand NAVER’s GAK Sejong AI factory from 55MW to 200MW by 2028. | source: How NAVER, NVIDIA & Brookfield Plan to Build a 200MW AI Hub (datacentremagazine.com) input gnews:bd132d31601aad1bc67616e1\n  - e1056 [third_party_view; 2026-07-27; push toward_last] NVIDIA’s planned NAVER investment is conditional on NAVER finalising at least US$9 billion of binding project financing. | source: How NAVER, NVIDIA & Brookfield Plan to Build a 200MW AI Hub (datacentremagazine.com) input gnews:bd132d31601aad1bc67616e1\n  - e1057 [fact; 2026-07-27; push toward_last] Brookfield executed a nonbinding term sheet to provide up to US$9 billion in project funding for the GAK Sejong expansion. | source: How NAVER, NVIDIA & Brookfield Plan to Build a 200MW AI Hub (datacentremagazine.com) input gnews:bd132d31601aad1bc67616e1\n  - e1058 [rumour; 2026-07-27; push toward_first] NAVER plans to provide the remaining capital required to complete construction of the expanded facility. | source: How NAVER, NVIDIA & Brookfield Plan to Build a 200MW AI Hub (datacentremagazine.com) input gnews:bd132d31601aad1bc67616e1\n  - e1120 [fact; 2026-07-28; push toward_first] NVIDIA announced a $1 billion investment in Naver on July 24, 2026, as part of a partnership to help build South Korea's sovereign AI infrastructure. | source: Nvidia's circular web of AI deals has investors asking an uncomfortable question (businessinsider.com) input gnews:52059ca6cf757271948579f5\n  - e1217 [party_statement; 2026-07-29; push toward_first] Nvidia said it planned to invest $1 billion in Naver to help finance its South Korean AI data center, allowing Naver to more than triple the facility's size; the site was to use Nvidia hardware. | source: Nvidia’s $750-billion AI bet deepens fears of a circular tech bubble (latimes.com) input gnews:c46e192520be10cb02a54ca8\n  - e1237 [fact; 2026-07-29; push toward_first] NVIDIA announced a $1 billion investment in Naver and a deal with SK Group to fund 2 GW of South Korean AI data-center facilities using NVIDIA’s DSX architecture. | source: Why NVIDIA's OpenAI Backstop Missed the Mark (futuriom.com) input gnews:e6eab7a05d5c77130cb231ca\n  - e1274 [fact; 2026-07-29; push toward_first] NVIDIA agreed to invest $1 billion in Naver to support expansion of sovereign AI infrastructure through the GAK Sejong data center. | source: Nvidia Stock Outlook: Is There Still Room to Rally After AI Expansion? (equiti.com) input gnews:4b34a84a492cb836ad1ba1ed\n- A random sample of uncited evidence in force (seed 20261120):\n  - e1055 [rumour; 2026-07-27] NVIDIA plans a US$1 billion strategic equity investment in NAVER Corp. | source: How NAVER, NVIDIA & Brookfield Plan to Build a 200MW AI Hub (datacentremagazine.com) input gnews:bd132d31601aad1bc67616e1\n  - e910 [party_statement; 2026-07-25] NVIDIA said it plans to invest $1 billion in Naver, which is building data centers around NVIDIA GPUs. | source: Nvidia locks down memory supply from SK Hynix as part of $500 billion AI deal (cnbc.com) input gnews:7cfba917f3742f912269c148\n  - e911 [party_statement; 2026-07-25] NVIDIA said the Naver project would let potential customers secure AI computing capacity before 200 megawatts of capacity is completed. | source: Nvidia locks down memory supply from SK Hynix as part of $500 billion AI deal (cnbc.com) input gnews:7cfba917f3742f912269c148\n  - e1117 [rumour; 2026-07-27] NVIDIA was separately investing US$1 billion in Naver to help finance an AI data centre under construction. | source: Nvidia Corp explores support for OpenAI (thestar.com.my) input gnews:cf24fe6341ca980677ca786e\n  - e944 [party_statement; 2026-07-25] Nvidia plans to invest US$1 billion in Naver to help finance a data centre under construction in South Korea. | source: Nvidia to invest US$1 billion in Naver and expand SK Group accord (businesstimes.com.sg) input gnews:99a1ba68b933cb3eeb687120\n\n## v33: What gross margin will NVIDIA report for FY2028?\n\n- Looks at: NVIDIA FY2028 gross margin\n- Opened 2026-08-26 from: ‘How the Heck’ Can Nvidia Grow Revenue 70% From Here? Jensen Huang Tells Jim Cramer ‘It’s Not Easy’ (finance.yahoo.com); due 2028-02-28; open\n- Research groups: none\n- Answer ladder:\n  1. Strongly bullish: FY2028 gross margin is at least 75%.\n  2. Bullish: FY2028 gross margin is at least 73% and below 75%.\n  3. Neutral: FY2028 gross margin is at least 71% and below 73%.  <== current\n  4. Bearish: FY2028 gross margin is at least 69% and below 71%.\n  5. Strongly bearish: FY2028 gross margin is below 69%.\n- History: 24 judgements; first judged 2026-08-26 at rung 3; rung moves: none\n- Latest judgement 2026-09-20, why changed: The rung and implied midpoint are unchanged, but the supporting risk picture has changed: NVIDIA says memory-price increases exceeded expectations and are headed higher, while SK Hynix expects the shortage to worsen and KB Securities estimates only 10 days of inventory. These add downside risk but do not quantify a change to NVIDIA’s FY2028 guidance.\n- Latest reasoning: The reference is NVIDIA management’s FY2028 gross-margin guidance of 72%–73%, which sits at rung 3. The implied outcome remains about 72.5%, the midpoint of that direct FY2028 guidance. The guided path—75% in FY2027 Q2, 74% in Q3, and 71%–72% in Q4 before recovery—supports the forecast, while worsening memory-cost and supply signals add downside risk without quantifying a further FY2028 reduction. The implied figure remains within 71%–below 73%, so it does not cross a rung boundary.\n- Evidence in force: 48 (party_statement 35, third_party_view 11, fact 2)\n- Evidence cited by the latest judgement:\n  - e2756 [party_statement; 2026-08-26; push none] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said gross margin was expected to bottom at 71–72% in FY2027 Q4 and settle at 72–73% in FY2028 as price increases take effect in Q1. | source: ‘How the Heck’ Can Nvidia Grow Revenue 70% From Here? Jensen Huang Tells Jim Cramer ‘It’s Not Easy’ (finance.yahoo.com) input gnews:81226ce463750079ebf335c5\n  - e3030 [fact; 2026-08-27; push toward_last] NVIDIA reported fiscal Q2 GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins of 75.0%. | source: NVIDIA (NVDA) Excluded China Data Center Compute Revenue from its $108B Outlook. Can Growth Stay Exceptional Without It? (insidermonkey.com) input gnews:ef1c09ba4c3333679aa5e869\n  - e3038 [party_statement; 2026-08-27; push toward_last] NVIDIA guided to fiscal Q3 GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins of 74.0%, plus or minus 0.5 percentage points. | source: NVIDIA (NVDA) Excluded China Data Center Compute Revenue from its $108B Outlook. Can Growth Stay Exceptional Without It? (insidermonkey.com) input gnews:ef1c09ba4c3333679aa5e869\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"jq '.views[] | select(.view_id==\\\"v9\\\") | {j:(.judgements[-1]|keys), e:(.evidence[0]|keys), moves:(.switches|type)}' /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad/api-library.json; sed -n '1,49p' docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"jq '.views[] | select(.view_id==\\\"v9\\\") | {j:(.judgements[-1]|keys), e:(.evidence[0]|keys), moves:(.switches|type)}' /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad/api-library.json; sed -n '1,49p' docs/prd/10-filing-phase.md\"","aggregated_output":"{\n  \"j\": [\n    \"changed\",\n    \"cited\",\n    \"cited_views\",\n    \"day\",\n    \"from_item\",\n    \"id\",\n    \"pushes\",\n    \"reasons\",\n    \"rung\",\n    \"seq\",\n    \"why_changed\"\n  ],\n  \"e\": [\n    \"arrival_day\",\n    \"day\",\n    \"event_time\",\n    \"id\",\n    \"linked_seq\",\n    \"one_line\",\n    \"original_day\",\n    \"original_who\",\n    \"outlets\",\n    \"quote\",\n    \"replaced_by\",\n    \"replaces\",\n    \"seq\",\n    \"source\",\n    \"sources\",\n    \"speaker\",\n    \"tier\"\n  ],\n  \"moves\": \"array\"\n}\n# Core PRD for the next phase: filing must recognise indirect relations the way an analyst does\n\n> Status: in progress (opened 2026-11-04). Alex: \"你把这个写好成下一阶段核心PRD然后不要drift, 把所有需要的context 全部充分记录，每次开始前都需要revisit, 然后开始做\".\n> This page is the single source for this phase. In a conflict: Alex's words → `docs/DECISIONS.md` → this page → the other design documents. This page's\n> conclusions, evidence, constraints, measuring method and process are not rewritten to follow test results;\n> test results go into `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/log.md`, and changing this page first needs Alex's word.\n> Amended 2026-11-04 (Alex replied \"go\" to the proposed amendment; D360): §5.2 drops \"also file under the other party's question\" and puts two\n> sentences in its place, one on choosing and one on splitting; §6's decision rule is rewritten. How it came about:\n> `docs/research/2026-11-04-filing-end-to-end/prd-amendment-proposal.md` and `log.md` rows 11–16.\n> 2026-11-05 (D368): translated into English on Alex's word (\"不，用英文吧\"); §4 corrected to name the frozen standard's version 5 and §5 given a status line, on his delegation (\"你要来帮我看到底做不做\").\n\n## 0. Checklist before every start (Alex: \"每次开始前都需要revisit\")\n\nBefore starting any step, finish these in order; if one cannot be done, do not start:\n\n1. Read §§1–8 of this page in full; read the last three rows of `log.md`, and confirm where the previous step stopped and why.\n2. Check against §3, \"not in scope\": what this step does is not in that table. If it is in the table, stop and take it to Alex.\n3. Check against §4, \"constraints\": this step changes nothing frozen; a change to the frozen extraction prompt is the one place §5 names, and Alex's\n   word for it is already given.\n4. If this step costs money: its predictions and stop conditions are written down and frozen as §7 says (with their sha256 recorded beside them); the\n   requests are rebuilt byte for byte as §6 says; the control is re-sent the same day.\n5. What goes to Alex: one conclusion, one recommendation, one test, after §7's rounds of review; no menu of options; in plain words (the opening of\n   AGENTS, \"Explain and answer as to a sixteen-year-old\").\n\n## 1. Goal (end to end)\n\nEach question's judgement must receive the facts that can change its answer; facts that no question receives, those that bear on no question, must\nnot be piled into some one question. What is measured is the final judgement (the frozen judgement ruler, reader B, at the window end 2026-07-14), not\nany one step's own score.\n\n## 2. The core blocker (proven)\n\n**The system uses a fact for a question only when the fact \"states exactly this question's own figure\". An analyst also reasons onward from a fact: the other party's figure in a deal, price and volume for a product's revenue, a product's variants for its launch, and so on. The filing step cannot do this; the judging step reasons onward once it has the facts.**\n\nThis one blocker has two faces (described earlier as \"two problems\"):\n\n- **The parking lot.** Filing does not recognise facts that bear on a question indirectly, so no question takes them; yet to be kept, a fact must\n  first be filed (D209, D264). So D273 added the fallback sentence, which parks them in NVIDIA's total-revenue question. Of the reports under the\n  total-revenue question, in the part the gold read, 32–39% are ones the standard does not keep, 31–44% are kept but used by none of the 22 questions,\n  and only 7–10% are what the gold actually uses to judge total revenue (three draws); 24–44% of the total-revenue question's judgement citations are\n  news about other companies or the whole market (Intel graphics-card retail prices, a gas-fired power plant in Taiwan).\n- **Narrow questions starve.** Questions that only indirect facts can answer do not receive them: the custom-chip question received none of Broadcom's\n  figures in any of the three draws, and judged \"below $40 billion\" in all three; the operating-margin and NVIDIA capital-spending questions received\n  nothing at all and were never judged.\n\n**Evidence:**\n\n| Evidence | Content | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import json, pathlib\ndata=json.loads(pathlib.Path('\\\\''/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad/api-library.json'\\\\'').read_text())\nvs={v['\\\\''view_id'\\\\'']:v for v in data['\\\\''views'\\\\'']}\nspec={'\\\\''v4'\\\\'':['\\\\''e2541'\\\\'','\\\\''e2611'\\\\'','\\\\''e4840'\\\\'','\\\\''e2319'\\\\''], '\\\\''v5'\\\\'':['\\\\''e1703'\\\\'','\\\\''e1925'\\\\'','\\\\''e2269'\\\\'','\\\\''e4696'\\\\'','\\\\''e4738'\\\\'','\\\\''e1091'\\\\'','\\\\''e1373'\\\\'','\\\\''e1490'\\\\''], '\\\\''v6'\\\\'':['\\\\''e1926'\\\\'','\\\\''e2535'\\\\''], '\\\\''v9'\\\\'':['\\\\''e2517'\\\\'','\\\\''e2574'\\\\'','\\\\''e3219'\\\\'','\\\\''e976'\\\\'','\\\\''e4765'\\\\'','\\\\''e4996'\\\\''], '\\\\''v14'\\\\'':['\\\\''e2497'\\\\'','\\\\''e2499'\\\\'','\\\\''e3943'\\\\'','\\\\''e4762'\\\\'','\\\\''e4901'\\\\''], '\\\\''v28'\\\\'':['\\\\''e2498'\\\\'','\\\\''e2569'\\\\'','\\\\''e2625'\\\\'','\\\\''e2627'\\\\'','\\\\''e3154'\\\\''], '\\\\''v34'\\\\'':['\\\\''e3768'\\\\'','\\\\''e5086'\\\\''], '\\\\''v43'\\\\'':['\\\\''e5087'\\\\'','\\\\''e5088'\\\\'','\\\\''e5089'\\\\'','\\\\''e5090'\\\\'']}\nfor v, ids in spec.items():\n for e in vs[v]['\\\\''evidence'\\\\'']:\n  if e['\\\\''id'\\\\''] not in ids: continue\n  print(e['\\\\''id'\\\\''],e['\\\\''quote'\\\\''])'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import json, pathlib\ndata=json.loads(pathlib.Path('\\\\''/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad/api-library.json'\\\\'').read_text())\nvs={v['\\\\''view_id'\\\\'']:v for v in data['\\\\''views'\\\\'']}\nspec={'\\\\''v4'\\\\'':['\\\\''e2541'\\\\'','\\\\''e2611'\\\\'','\\\\''e4840'\\\\'','\\\\''e2319'\\\\''], '\\\\''v5'\\\\'':['\\\\''e1703'\\\\'','\\\\''e1925'\\\\'','\\\\''e2269'\\\\'','\\\\''e4696'\\\\'','\\\\''e4738'\\\\'','\\\\''e1091'\\\\'','\\\\''e1373'\\\\'','\\\\''e1490'\\\\''], '\\\\''v6'\\\\'':['\\\\''e1926'\\\\'','\\\\''e2535'\\\\''], '\\\\''v9'\\\\'':['\\\\''e2517'\\\\'','\\\\''e2574'\\\\'','\\\\''e3219'\\\\'','\\\\''e976'\\\\'','\\\\''e4765'\\\\'','\\\\''e4996'\\\\''], '\\\\''v14'\\\\'':['\\\\''e2497'\\\\'','\\\\''e2499'\\\\'','\\\\''e3943'\\\\'','\\\\''e4762'\\\\'','\\\\''e4901'\\\\''], '\\\\''v28'\\\\'':['\\\\''e2498'\\\\'','\\\\''e2569'\\\\'','\\\\''e2625'\\\\'','\\\\''e2627'\\\\'','\\\\''e3154'\\\\''], '\\\\''v34'\\\\'':['\\\\''e3768'\\\\'','\\\\''e5086'\\\\''], '\\\\''v43'\\\\'':['\\\\''e5087'\\\\'','\\\\''e5088'\\\\'','\\\\''e5089'\\\\'','\\\\''e5090'\\\\'']}\nfor v, ids in spec.items():\n for e in vs[v]['\\\\''evidence'\\\\'']:\n  if e['\\\\''id'\\\\''] not in ids: continue\n  print(e['\\\\''id'\\\\''],e['\\\\''quote'\\\\''])'\"","aggregated_output":"e2319 Some of Nvidia's largest customers have been told that systems containing Grace Blackwell and next-generation Vera Rubin chips will become more expensive when shipments begin early next year, Bloomberg reported. The increases will vary depending on the chip generation and memory configuration.\ne2541 We commenced production shipments of Vera Rubin earlier this month, having already received purchase orders from every major hyperscaler, AI cloud and system OEM, we expect Vera Rubin to mark the fastest product ramp in NVIDIA's history.\ne2611 Our next-generation Data Center architecture, Vera Rubin, began production shipments in the third quarter of fiscal year 2027.\ne4840 CoreWeave has brought seven Nvidia Vera Rubin NVL72 racks into production across two regions, totaling 504 GPUs, as the AI cloud provider begins scaling Nvidia's newest systems beyond a single rack.\ne1091 Alphabet released second-quarter results on July 22 and announced that it is raising its 2026 capex guidance to a range of $195 billion to $205 billion. The company had earlier projected $180 billion to $190 billion in capex for 2026.\ne1373 Meta Platforms ( NASDAQ:META ) raised its FY26 capex guidance to $125 billion to $145 billion\ne1490 Amazon (NASDAQ: AMZN) is doing the same, going from an estimated $200 billion to a new projection of $220 billion, although, in fairness, the increase was mostly due to higher memory chip costs.\ne1703 Combined 2026 capital spending across the four largest hyperscalers is tracking near $745 billion\ne1925 Microsoft, Amazon, Alphabet, Meta and other hyperscalers whose cloud platforms host most of the world's AI workloads have together guided roughly $720 billion (€624bn) to $745 billion (€646bn) of capital spending in 2026, an increase of about 77% on last year.\ne2269 The top four hyperscalers plan $760 billion in capex in 2026 alone.\ne4696 Four top hyperscalers — Amazon, Alphabet, Microsoft and Meta Platforms — alone are expected to spend roughly $720 billion to $745 billion on capital expenditures in 2026, with a large portion directed toward AI infrastructure.\ne4738 Reuters cited BofA Global Research estimates showing Microsoft, Alphabet ( NASDAQ:GOOG ), Amazon ( NASDAQ:AMZN ), Meta ( NASDAQ:META ) and Oracle ( NYSE:ORCL ) could collectively pour roughly $795 billion into capital expenditures in 2026\ne1926 What analysts expect the hyperscalers to spend in 2027 alone has more than doubled in the space of a year, from a consensus of $480 billion (€416bn) in August 2025 to $1.08 trillion (€943bn) this month, a rise of about 127%, according to Bank of America.\ne2535 With cloud industry backlog now greater than $2 trillion, CapEx by the top 5 hyperscalers is expected to reach nearly $800 billion in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\ne976 And even though SemiAnalysis reported the delays, it's still bullish on Nvidia, forecasting that the chipmaker's data center revenue will exceed analyst estimates by 20% in the second half of its fiscal 2027.\ne2517 Data Center revenue of $89.0 billion, up 117% from a year ago\ne2574 Data Center revenue for the second quarter was a record $89.0 billion, up 117% from a year ago and up 18% sequentially, driven by the ramp of our Blackwell Ultra infrastructure.\ne3219 Hold the data center mix at last quarter's 92.5%, and data center revenue works out to $99.9 billion -- about $100 million short of the line.\ne4765 ACIE already accounts for about 45% of Nvidia's Data Center revenue. Management expects ACIE to drive most of the sequential revenue growth for Data Center in the third quarter, while hyperscaler demand could pick up more strongly in the fourth quarter and in fiscal 2028.\ne4996 That translates into an annual run rate of just under $330 billion for the full year.\ne2497 NVIDIA (NASDAQ: NVDA) today reported revenue for the second quarter ended July 26, 2026, of $96.2 billion, up 18% from the previous quarter and up 106% from a year ago.\ne2499 Revenue is expected to be $108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%. NVIDIA is not assuming any Data Center compute revenue from China in its outlook.\ne3943 In the meantime, Nvidia's fiscal 2027 third-quarter guidance implies 12% sequential growth at the midpoint.\ne4762 Nvidia has exceeded the high end of its revenue guidance in each of the past 12 quarters. Over the past four quarters, actual revenue has averaged 3.1% above the high end of guidance.\ne4901 This suggests that Nvidia could leave demand unfulfilled for more than $100 billion in AI infrastructure gear, which includes its GPUs as well as other kinds of chips it designs, such as CPUs and DPUs, that are becoming more pervasive in its systems.\ne2498 For the quarter, GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins were both 75.0%.\ne2569 For Q3, we expect GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins to be 74% plus or minus 50 basis points. We expect margins to bottom in Q4 in the 71% to 72% range before settling at 72% to 73% in fiscal year '28, as executed price increases take effect in Q1.\ne2625 During the first half of fiscal year 2027, we incurred a $0.4 billion charge associated with H200 for excess inventory and purchase obligations, as the demand for H200 products diminished.\ne2627 As a result, any H200s shipped under the new licensing program are subject to a 25% tariff upon importation into the United States. We have been unable to pass along any of the tariff to our customers, and do not anticipate doing so in the event we are able to sell licensed products into the China market.\ne3154 These sales represented less than 1% of data center revenue, and Kress flagged those units as \"dilutive to corporate gross margin.\"\ne3768 On September 2, 2026, NVIDIA Corporation (“NVIDIA”) entered into a definitive agreement to acquire Hugging Face, Inc.\ne5086 A definitive agreement was signed on September 2, 2026, and CEO Jensen Huang disclosed the deal in an official blog post on September 3.\ne5087 NVIDIA (NVDA) announced it will acquire U.S.-based open-source AI platform Hugging Face for approximately $12.9 billion (¥2 trillion)\ne5088 Deal completion is targeted for the first half of 2027, but approvals from regulators in multiple jurisdictions, including the EU, are also required.\ne5089 The transaction meets the mandatory filing threshold under the Hart-Scott-Rodino Antitrust Improvements Act, requiring notification to the U.S. Federal Trade Commission (FTC) and the Department of Justice.\ne5090 UK technology publication The Register published an article titled \"Hugging Face is too important to fall into NVIDIA's hands,\" arguing that regulators should intervene.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
