{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import json, pathlib\ndata=json.loads(pathlib.Path('\\\\''/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad/api-library.json'\\\\'').read_text())\nvs={v['\\\\''view_id'\\\\'']:v for v in data['\\\\''views'\\\\'']}\nimport sqlite3, re\nconn=sqlite3.connect('\\\\''file:poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/items-comparison.sqlite?mode=ro'\\\\'',uri=True)\nids=dict(re.findall(r'\\\\''\"'^  - (e'\"\\\\d+) \\\\[.*? input (\\\\S+)\"'$'\"'\\\\'',pathlib.Path('\\\\''docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/council-q/library-pack.md'\\\\'').read_text(),re.M))\nes={e['\\\\''id'\\\\'']:e for v in data['\\\\''views'\\\\''] for e in v['\\\\''evidence'\\\\'']}\n\nfor v in ('\\\\''v14'\\\\'','\\\\''v9'\\\\'','\\\\''v5'\\\\'','\\\\''v6'\\\\'','\\\\''v28'\\\\'','\\\\''v4'\\\\'','\\\\''v34'\\\\'','\\\\''v43'\\\\'','\\\\''v10'\\\\'','\\\\''v15'\\\\'','\\\\''v17'\\\\'','\\\\''v21'\\\\'','\\\\''v35'\\\\''):\n js=vs[v]['\\\\''judgements'\\\\'']\n print(v, len(js))\n for i,j in enumerate(js):\n  if i and j['\\\\''rung'\\\\'']==js[i-1]['\\\\''rung'\\\\'']: continue\n  print(j['\\\\''day'\\\\''], j['\\\\''rung'\\\\''], (j['\\\\''why_changed'\\\\''] or '\\\\'''\\\\'')[:480], (j['\\\\''reasons'\\\\''] or '\\\\'''\\\\'')[:355])\n'\"","aggregated_output":"v14 71\n2026-07-10 3 This is the first judgement. Bloomberg’s $393 billion FY2027 sales projection sits in rung 3, just below the $400 billion rung-2 boundary. The in-force themes are supportive of NVIDIA demand, but supply no quantified revenue adjustment that takes the forecast across that boundary; the most likely outcome therefore remains in rung 3.\n2026-07-26 2 The implied outcome has moved from about $387 billion to roughly $400–$420 billion, crossing the $400 billion boundary into rung 2. The key change is the additional quantified bookings and the Blackwell/Vera Rubin sales forecast, balanced against ongoing supply constraints. The reference is about $393 billion for FY2027, based on the Bloomberg/Wall Street estimate and roughly 82% growth from FY2026; that is rung 3. The Q1 result and $91 billion Q2 guide, carrying forward the roughly 11.5% sequential growth implied by guidance for Q3 and Q4, give a base path of about $387 billion. I raise that path modestly: NVIDIA reported\n2026-08-11 3 The rung changes from 2 to 3, and the implied FY2027 estimate changes from about $410 billion to about $392.5 billion. The Q3 estimate of $103.1 billion gives a more concrete trajectory for the remaining quarters; carrying its sequential growth into Q4 yields a figure below $400 billion, while demand and order evidence does not quantify enough FY2027 revenue to offset that run-rate. The market reference is about $393 billion for FY2027, which is rung 3. The reported Q1 revenue, Q2 guidance, and specific Q3 analyst estimate imply about $392.5 billion if Q4 continues Q2-to-Q3 sequential growth: $81.6B + $91B + $103.1B + approximately $116.8B. Strong demand indicators support the run-rate, but the cited large order and infrastructure \n2026-08-19 2 The forecast moves from rung 3 to rung 2 because the newer, quantified BofA estimates for Q2 and potential Q3 guidance, together with Q1's reported beat versus its earlier guidance, imply an FY2027 total above $400 billion rather than the prior approximately $391 billion path. The market reference is approximately $393 billion for FY2027, which is rung 3; Zacks’ 80% growth estimate applied to FY2026 revenue also implies roughly $389 billion, rung 3. The latest operating path instead points just above $400 billion: the Q2 management guide is $91 billion, but BofA expects $94–95 billion; BofA also sees potential Q3 guidance of \nv9 74\n2026-07-06 2 This is the first judgement. The evidence implies about $410 billion, within rung 2 ($400 billion to below $450 billion). This is a forecast, not an established FY2027 result: it carries the observed 21% sequential growth from Q1 through the remaining three quarters. The resulting estimate is above $400 billion but does not cross the $450 billion boundary. No consensus or company F\n2026-07-10 3 The rung changes from 2 to 3, and the implied FY2027 figure changes from the previous roughly $420 billion estimate to roughly $357 billion. The new $91 billion Q2 total-revenue guidance constrains the prior extrapolation of 21% sequential Data Center growth; applying the Q1 segment mix and carrying forward the growth rate implied by total-revenue guidance produces an estimate below $400 billion. The supplied evidence gives no FY2027 Data Center consensus or segment guidance, so there is no direct market-reference rung. The nearest company anchor is $91 billion of total Q2 FY2027 revenue guidance, not a FY2027 segment forecast. Using Q1 Data Center’s 92% share of total revenue as a proxy implies about $83.8 billion of Q2 Data Center revenue; car\n2026-07-12 2 The new management statement of roughly $1T in Blackwell and Vera Rubin revenue visibility, together with the Q2 growth-acceleration statement, changes the implied FY2027 outcome from the prior roughly $357B run-rate estimate to about $400–410B, crossing into rung 2. No FY2027 Data Center consensus or segment guidance is supplied. The closest company reference is the $91B Q2 FY2027 total-revenue guide; applying Q1 Data Center's roughly 92% share and carrying the guide-implied 11.5% sequential growth forward gives about $357B, which is rung 3. The forecast is higher: Q1 Data Center revenue grew 21% sequentially, mana\n2026-07-15 3 The rung moves from 2 to 3, and the supporting forecast is revised from about $420B to about $385B. The newly supplied FY2026 base and Q1 growth rate imply about $372B when carried forward; the Q2 guide and acceleration signal add upside, but do not support the prior estimate above $400B. The closest reference is NVIDIA’s approximately $91B Q2 FY2027 total-revenue guide. Applying the Q1 Data Center share of 92% gives a rough Q2 Data Center reference of $83.7B; carrying the implied Q1-to-Q2 sequential increase through the remaining quarters produces about $356B for FY2027, which is rung 3. The forecast is higher, about $385B: FY2026 Data \n2026-07-22 2 The forecast moves from the previous roughly $395B estimate to about $405B and crosses the $400B boundary. The updated 2026 hyperscaler capex theme and recent evidence of accelerating demand and a faster Rubin ramp support a modest higher forecast; competition and China risks limit the increase. The FY2027 reference is about $372B, rung 3: NVIDIA has not supplied full-year Data Center guidance, so carrying FY2026 Data Center revenue forward at the reported 92% Q1 FY2027 growth rate is the available annual reference. The $91B Q2 total-revenue guide, with no China Data Center revenue assumed, is a near-term check rather than a full-year forecast.\n2026-08-07 1 The forecast moves from rung 2 to rung 1 as the newer evidence supports an implied outcome above $450B, rather than the previous estimate of about $445B. The $1T Blackwell/Rubin visibility is discounted because it spans products and calendar years and is not FY2027 revenue guidance; the shift also relies on the near-term growth outlook and more specific customer and demand signals. The reference is rung 3: no FY2027 Data Center consensus or full-year guidance is supplied, and carrying FY2026 Data Center revenue of $193.7B forward at the reported 92% growth rate implies about $372B. The forecast is about $460B: the near-term revenue trajectory and accelerating-growth guidance are reinforced by specific shipment tracking, large cust\n2026-08-08 2 The rung moves from 1 to 2 and the implied figure changes from about $460B to about $420B. The reported $75.25B Q1 Data Center result and $91B Q2 total-revenue guidance are strong, but the evidence in view does not support retaining an estimate above $450B after accounting for the competing AMD deployments and the limited direct revenue implication of the large SK headline. No FY2027 Data Center consensus or full-year guidance is supplied. The reference is therefore the FY2026 Data Center revenue of $193.7B carried forward at the latest reported 92% year-over-year growth, or about $372B—rung 3. The evidence supports an outcome above that reference: Q2 total-revenue guidance is $91B and management expects Data Center growth\n2026-09-15 3 The rung changes from 2 to 3 because the latest Q2 result and Q3 revenue guide, carried forward with the stated Q4 acceleration, imply roughly $379 billion rather than the prior approximately $414 billion estimate. The reference is an approximately $414 billion FY2027 expectation, which sits in rung 2. The latest reported quarter and near-term outlook imply a lower run-rate: Q1 and Q2 Data Center revenue total $164.25 billion; applying the recent 92.5% Data Center mix to NVIDIA's $108 billion Q3 total-revenue guide gives about $100 billion for Q3. Carrying that gr\n2026-09-20 2 The rung moves from 3 to 2 because the quantified SemiAnalysis forecast for second-half revenue above analyst estimates lifts the implied FY2027 outcome across $400 billion, while the Q3 guide and first-half reported revenue anchor the estimate. The updated China-authorization theme adds some upside possibility, but NVIDIA's stated no-China-revenue outlook prevents treating that as booked revenue. The market reference is approximately $414 billion for FY2027, which is rung 2. The evidence-based estimate is now around $410 billion: NVIDIA reported $164 billion in the first half, Q3 total-revenue guidance and the recent Data Center mix imply roughly $100 billion for Q3, and a roughly $146 billion Q4 is supported by the expected hyperscale reacceler\nv5 56\n2026-07-04 3 This is the first judgement. The stated plan of around $650 billion falls within rung 3 ($600 billion to below $700 billion). No additional evidence in vi