ate. The reference is the latest quantified reported amount, about $124 billion, which sits in rung 3 ($100 billion to below $150 billion); no separate FY2027 consensus or company guidance is supplied. The forecast is also rung 3: the recent reported amounts support a modest rise from that level, but do not support carrying the earlier sharp increase forward\n2026-07-27 4 The rung changes from 3 to 4. The forecast now carries the recent upward trajectory forward to roughly $160 billion; the possible SK Hynix agreement and updated AI-capex themes add upside, but the rumor is not treated as a confirmed $500 billion commitment. The reference is NVIDIA’s latest quantified disclosure, roughly $124 billion, which sits in rung 3; there is no FY2027 consensus or company guidance for this measure. The forecast is about $160 billion, crossing the $150 billion boundary into rung 4: recent commitment levels have risen, and the latest increase, continued expansion, and stronger AI-capex\n2026-08-01 3 The forecast moves from rung 4 to rung 3: the June figure of roughly $124 billion is a newer quantified level, while the $119 billion Q1 figure and its $95.2 billion prior-quarter comparison remain context. The implied FY2027-report outcome is now about $135–$140 billion rather than roughly $180 billion. No market consensus is supplied. The observable reference is NVIDIA's Q1 FY2027 reported $119 billion, with a roughly $124 billion June figure; both are in rung 3. The earlier increase from $95.2 billion to $119 billion supports continued growth, but the later $124 billion figure points to a more measured pace than repeating that prior increase. With no\n2026-08-06 4 The latest quantified figure is now $145 billion, materially above the prior $124 billion reference and close to the $150 billion rung boundary. A conservative partial carry-forward of the recent increase implies a year-end outcome above that boundary, changing the forecast from rung 3 to rung 4. There is no explicit ma