{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import json, pathlib\ndata=json.loads(pathlib.Path('\\\\''/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad/api-library.json'\\\\'').read_text())\nvs={v['\\\\''view_id'\\\\'']:v for v in data['\\\\''views'\\\\'']}\nimport sqlite3, re\nconn=sqlite3.connect('\\\\''file:poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/items-comparison.sqlite?mode=ro'\\\\'',uri=True)\nids=dict(re.findall(r'\\\\''\"'^  - (e'\"\\\\d+) \\\\[.*? input (\\\\S+)\"'$'\"'\\\\'',pathlib.Path('\\\\''docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/council-q/library-pack.md'\\\\'').read_text(),re.M))\nes={e['\\\\''id'\\\\'']:e for v in data['\\\\''views'\\\\''] for e in v['\\\\''evidence'\\\\'']}\n\nfor k, pat in {\n'\\\\''e2541'\\\\'':r'\\\\''commenced production shipments|top 5 hyperscalers|margins to bottom|0\\\\.7 billion|only supports|available supply'\\\\'',\n'\\\\''e2611'\\\\'':r'\\\\''began production shipments|\\\\\"'$279 billion|'\"\\\\\"'$0'\"\\\\.4 billion charge|25% tariff'\\\\'',\n'\\\\''e4840'\\\\'':r'\\\\''seven Nvidia Vera Rubin NVL72 racks|deployed|first production'\\\\'',\n'\\\\''e3768'\\\\'':r'\\\\''definitive agreement|11\\\\.9 billion|1\\\\.0 billion|first half'\\\\'',\n'\\\\''e5088'\\\\'':r'\\\\''targeted for the first half|mandatory filing threshold|intervene|12\\\\.[79] billion'\\\\'',\n'\\\\''e2319'\\\\'':r'\\\\''early next year'\\\\'',\n}.items():\n title, pub, day, t=conn.execute('\\\\''select title,publisher,publication_day,text from items where input_id=?'\\\\'',(ids[k],)).fetchone()\n print(k, ids[k],day,pub,title)\n print('\\\\''quote literal:'\\\\'', es[k]['\\\\''quote'\\\\''] in t)\n for m in list(re.finditer(pat,t,re.I))[:7]: print(re.sub(r'\\\\''\\\\s+'\\\\'','\\\\'' '\\\\'',t[max(0,m.start()-90):m.end()+100]))\n'\"","aggregated_output":"e2541 truenorth:transcript:NVDA:2026-08-26 2026-08-26 NVIDIA Corporation NVDA Q2 FY2027 Earnings Call\nquote literal: True\nexpanding margins. With cloud industry backlog now greater than $2 trillion, CapEx by the top 5 hyperscalers is expected to reach nearly $800 billion in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027. Today, we are delighted\nr throughput per megawatt and 35x lower token costs relative to Grace Blackwell Ultra. We commenced production shipments of Vera Rubin earlier this month, having already received purchase orders from every major hypersca\nexpect GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins to be 74% plus or minus 50 basis points. We expect margins to bottom in Q4 in the 71% to 72% range before settling at 72% to 73% in fiscal year '28, as executed price i\ne2611 edgar:NVDA:0001045810-26-000075:nvda-20260726.htm 2026-08-26 NVIDIA Corporation NVDA 10-Q 10-Q\nquote literal: True\njority of our system shipments. Our next-generation Data Center architecture, Vera Rubin, began production shipments in the third quarter of fiscal year 2027. We will be shipping both Blackwell and Rubin systems in t\nificantly increased our supply and capacity commitments from $119 billion last quarter to $279 billion as of July 26, 2026 to meet future demand. Refer to Note 10 of the Notes to Condensed Consolidated \nucts for which we have licenses. During the first half of fiscal year 2027, we incurred a $0.4 billion charge associated with H200 for excess inventory and purchase obligations, as the demand for H200 products\ncustomer. As a result, any H200s shipped under the new licensing program are subject to a 25% tariff upon importation into the United States. We have been unable to pass along any of the tariff to our\nucts for which we have licenses. During the first half of fiscal year 2027, we incurred a $0.4 billion charge associated with H200 for excess inventory and purchase obligations, as the demand for H200 products\ncustomer. As a result, any H200s shipped under the new licensing program are subject to a 25% tariff upon importation into the United States. We have been unable to pass along any of the tariff to our\ne4840 gnews:8392653145e1e67a11ab3654 2026-09-16 techtarget.com With Nvidia GPUs, CoreWeave hits architectural milestone\nquote literal: True\nck capabilities into production around Nvidia's latest AI platform. CoreWeave has brought seven Nvidia Vera Rubin NVL72 racks into production across two regions, totaling 504 GPUs, as the AI cloud provider begins scaling Nvid\nail. That figure is an architectural-scale target, rather than a claim that CoreWeave has deployed a 128,000 GPU Rubin cluster. The company has disclosed 504 Rubin GPUs across its seven production r\ne3768 edgar:NVDA:0001045810-26-000078:nvda-20260902.htm 2026-09-03 NVIDIA Corporation NVDA 8-K 8-K\nquote literal: True\nem 8.01. Other Events. On September 2, 2026, NVIDIA Corporation (“NVIDIA”) entered into a definitive agreement to acquire Hugging Face, Inc. (“Hugging Face”). Hugging Face operates a platform and community for \nopen-source models, datasets and applications. The transaction includes an approximately $11.9 billion purchase price payable to Hugging Face stockholders, subject to certain adjustments, and an equity-\nect to certain adjustments, and an equity-based retention program of up to approximately $1.0 billion for Hugging Face employees joining NVIDIA. The transaction is expected to close in the first half o\non for Hugging Face employees joining NVIDIA. The transaction is expected to close in the first half of 2027, subject to the satisfaction or waiver of customary closing conditions, including receipt o\ne5088 gnews:7a5002df93385ecef12a2cd8 2026-09-20 finance.biggo.com NVIDIA to Acquire Hugging Face for Approximately $12.7 Billion in Strategic Pivot After Earlier Investment Rejection\nquote literal: True\nNVIDIA to Acquire Hugging Face for Approximately $12.7 Billion in Strategic Pivot After Earlier Investment Rejection U.S. semiconductor giant NVIDIA announced it \n NVIDIA announced it will acquire open-source AI platform Hugging Face for approximately $12.9 billion (¥2 trillion). Hugging Face CEO Clément Delangue, who had rejected a roughly $500 million investmen\nounced it will acquire U.S.-based open-source AI platform Hugging Face for approximately $12.9 billion (¥2 trillion). A definitive agreement was signed on September 2, 2026, and CEO Jensen Huang disclos\n annualized revenue is estimated at approximately $150 million (¥24 billion), making the $12.9 billion acquisition price equivalent to roughly 86 times price-to-sales ratio (PSR)—an extraordinary multip\nle itself could shake that foundation. On the regulatory front, the transaction meets the mandatory filing threshold under the Hart-Scott-Rodino Antitrust Improvements Act, requiring notification to the U.S. Federal \nthe U.S. Federal Trade Commission (FTC) and the Department of Justice. Deal completion is targeted for the first half of 2027, but approvals from regulators in multiple jurisdictions, including the EU, are also requir\nugging Face is too important to fall into NVIDIA's hands,\" arguing that regulators should intervene. Competitors are likely to file objections with antitrust authorities, and the U.S. Department of J\ne2319 gnews:ee7e42e28c5cf342934099bd 2026-08-24 gurufocus.com Nvidia Makes Aggressive Move Ahead of Earnings\nquote literal: True\nwell and next-generation Vera Rubin chips will become more expensive when shipments begin early next year, Bloomberg reported. The increases will vary depending on the chip generation and memory configurat\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"agent_message","text":"The two revenue cards disagree in a way that matters. The Data Center card projects more fourth-quarter revenue for the segment than the total-revenue card projects for the whole company. I’ll verify the inputs and check which event calls are backed by filings."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import json, pathlib\ndata=json.loads(pathlib.Path('\\\\''/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad/api-library.json'\\\\'').read_text())\nvs={v['\\\\''view_id'\\\\'']:v for v in data['\\\\''views'\\\\'']}\nimport sqlite3, re\nconn=sqlite3.connect('\\\\''file:poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/items-comparison.sqlite?mode=ro'\\\\'',uri=True)\nids=dict(re.findall(r'\\\\''\"'^  - (e'\"\\\\d+) \\\\[.*? input (\\\\S+)\"'$'\"'\\\\'',pathlib.Path('\\\\''docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/council-q/library-pack.md'\\\\'').read_text(),re.M))\nes={e['\\\\''id'\\\\'']:e for v in data['\\\\''views'\\\\''] for e in v['\\\\''evidence'\\\\'']}\n\nfor v in data['\\\\''views'\\\\'']:\n j=v['\\\\''judgements'\\\\''][-1] if v['\\\\''judgements'\\\\''] else {}\n if v['\\\\''view_id'\\\\''] in ('\\\\''v4'\\\\'','\\\\''v5'\\\\'','\\\\''v6'\\\\'','\\\\''v9'\\\\'','\\\\''v14'\\\\'','\\\\''v28'\\\\'','\\\\''v34'\\\\'','\\\\''v43'\\\\''): continue\n print(v['\\\\''view_id'\\\\''],(j.get('\\\\''reasons'\\\\'') or '\\\\'''\\\\'')[:520])\nprint('\\\\''margin midpoint'\\\\'',sum(x*y for x,y in zip((81.6,96.2,108,127),(74.9,75,74,71.5)))/412.8)\n'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import json, pathlib\ndata=json.loads(pathlib.Path('\\\\''/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad/api-library.json'\\\\'').read_text())\nvs={v['\\\\''view_id'\\\\'']:v for v in data['\\\\''views'\\\\'']}\nimport sqlite3, re\nconn=sqlite3.connect('\\\\''file:poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/items-comparison.sqlite?mode=ro'\\\\'',uri=True)\nids=dict(re.findall(r'\\\\''\"'^  - (e'\"\\\\d+) \\\\[.*? input (\\\\S+)\"'$'\"'\\\\'',pathlib.Path('\\\\''docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/council-q/library-pack.md'\\\\'').read_text(),re.M))\nes={e['\\\\''id'\\\\'']:e for v in data['\\\\''views'\\\\''] for e in v['\\\\''evidence'\\\\'']}\n\nfor v in data['\\\\''views'\\\\'']:\n j=v['\\\\''judgements'\\\\''][-1] if v['\\\\''judgements'\\\\''] else {}\n if v['\\\\''view_id'\\\\''] in ('\\\\''v4'\\\\'','\\\\''v5'\\\\'','\\\\''v6'\\\\'','\\\\''v9'\\\\'','\\\\''v14'\\\\'','\\\\''v28'\\\\'','\\\\''v34'\\\\'','\\\\''v43'\\\\''): continue\n print(v['\\\\''view_id'\\\\''],(j.get('\\\\''reasons'\\\\'') or '\\\\'''\\\\'')[:520])\nprint('\\\\''margin midpoint'\\\\'',sum(x*y for x,y in zip((81.6,96.2,108,127),(74.9,75,74,71.5)))/412.8)\n'\"","aggregated_output":"v1 The reference is NVIDIA’s disclosed $108.5 billion of AI-cloud guarantees and related agreements ($105 billion for SB Energy/PORTS-Pike plus $3.5 billion for other AI-cloud partners), which is rung 5. The implied amount is higher: NVIDIA said it would provide selective credit enhancement for nearly 2 GW for another frontier lab; scaling the $105 billion backstop for the reported 10 GW PORTS-Pike campus gives an indicative additional $21 billion, or roughly $129.5 billion total if additive. The estimate is uncertain\nv2 The reference is NVIDIA’s latest company-reported maximum exposure: $56 billion of AI-cloud and third-party lease commitments plus $108.5 billion of guarantee exposure, about $164.5 billion, which is rung 5. The forecast remains far above the $20 billion boundary. I modestly raise the implied level above that reported baseline because Bank of America estimates $230 billion of guarantees or backstops to date and Morgan Stanley forecasts about $200 billion of all-in credit exposure by calendar 2028; both are broader \nv3 The reference is the status quo—no signed guarantee—which is rung 1; no separate market consensus is supplied. NVIDIA disclosed that it entered into the SB Energy guarantees for leases serving OpenAI at the Ohio campus, so the evidence establishes the rung 2 outcome. The guarantees’ phased effectiveness, with the first phase expected in FY2029, concerns when coverage takes effect, not whether NVIDIA has signed by the 2027-07-01 deadline.\nv7 The reference is NVIDIA’s stated 2027 roadmap, which sits at rung 1 (Yes); third-party expectations also place Kyber in 2027. But the most thesis-specific forecast is SemiAnalysis’s report that Kyber NVL144 is delayed to 2028 because of midplane manufacturability. NVIDIA’s general roadmap statements, including the planned 2027 NVL576 domain, do not specifically resolve that reported delay. Memory constraints and rack-scale technical challenges add downside, while the AWS GPU commitment spans products and both 2027 \nv8 The reference is NVIDIA's roughly $20 billion FY2027 stand-alone CPU sales guidance, which sits at rung 2; no separate market-consensus figure is supplied. The evidence supports a substantial ramp but still implies about $18 billion: Wolfe's estimate of 1.3 million Vera chips at about $5,000 each is roughly $6.5 billion, while AWS's CPU quantity is only described as millions without a purchase period and OCI's hundreds-of-thousands plan is not a FY2027 revenue figure. These disclosures, alongside shipments underway\nv10 The reference sits at rung 2: NVIDIA's Q3 FY2027 guidance assumes no China Data Center compute revenue. That is a sales assumption, not a forecast that Chinese authorities will authorize no purchases. The evidence implies narrow, case-by-case authorization is more likely than no authorization by the deadline: reports describe permission for selected buyers and some deliveries, and a report says official exceptions were issued to named firms. The evidence that China mostly blocked imports and restricted sales under \nv11 The reference is the reported proposal for about US$500 million in preference shares, intended to convert at Firmus’s IPO later in 2026; that sits at rung 1 (Yes). NVIDIA’s follow-on investment in Firmus’s fully subscribed round is concrete evidence consistent with the proposed investment being completed, and the remaining time to the deadline supports Yes as the more likely outcome. The round disclosure does not specify NVIDIA’s amount or share class, so it does not establish that this particular preference-share \nv12 The reference sits at rung 1: broad reporting points to NVIDIA's next consumer-facing gaming GPUs arriving in 2027, though it does not specifically establish an RTX 60-series launch by year-end. The more specific timing reports point to 2028: the reported mass-production target has slipped from late 2027 to 2028, and the RTX 60-series itself is reportedly pushed to 2028. That makes no launch by 2027-12-31 more likely. The additional capacity-prioritization report supports that forecast; the report that manufacturin\nv13 The reference is rung 2: no market consensus or formal decision timetable is supplied, and the investigation had not been reported as concluded. The authority’s July statement that the investigation was “nearing a conclusion” makes a decision by the year-end deadline more likely than no decision, so the forecast remains at rung 1.\nv15 The disclosed schedule is the reference: $279 billion of commitments, including $92 billion scheduled for the rest of FY2027. At the FY2027 report publication, the schedule alone implies about $187 billion remaining, which is rung 4. I forecast about $215 billion: that $187 billion residual plus an estimated $28 billion of replenishment as NVIDIA secures supply for strong future demand. The CEO's forecast of twice as many chip sales in 2027 and the large recent increase in commitments support continued additions; c\nv16 The reference is NVIDIA’s approximately $20 billion FY2027 standalone CPU outlook, which sits at rung 3. The most likely total is just above $25 billion: the specific FY2027 Vera outlook is about $20 billion, and Grace had already exceeded $5 billion on a trailing-12-month basis during FY2027; carrying that recent Grace run-rate through the fiscal year puts the total slightly above the rung-1 boundary. Vera shipments and full production support the outlook, though the estimate is close to the boundary: Wolfe’s 2026\nv17 The company’s approximately 70% FY2028 growth guidance is the primary reference and sits at rung 1; the more recent analyst estimates of 63.2%–65.5% also sit at rung 1, while the 44% LSEG estimate is a rung-3 counterweight. The implied outcome remains about 70%: NVIDIA says supply supports that growth, while demand is higher. The newer forecast of twice as many chips sold in calendar 2027 supports strong volume but does not translate directly into FY2028 revenue growth or displace the company’s supply-constrained o\nv18 The reference is NVIDIA’s stated plan for Vera general availability in the second half of 2026, reinforced by the reported fall launch; that sits at rung 1 (Yes). The evidence now makes Yes the more likely outcome: NVIDIA has reported Vera in full production and shipping to customers, AWS has received a Vera server, and Ian Buck said Vera had been officially released. These facts support availability by the deadline, though they do not explicitly establish broad general availability. The binary forecast therefore r\nv19 No explicit market consensus or Senate timetable is supplied. The observable reference is that RASA had not passed the Senate as of August 19, which sits at rung 2. The alleged remote-access cases could add pressure for legislation, but the recent evidence reports no Senate action or scheduled vote. On balance, non-passage by December 31 remains more likely.\nv20 The reference is NVIDIA’s announced plan to invest $1 billion, which sits at rung 1 (Yes). The evidence still makes completion by 2027-07-24 more likely than non-completion: NVIDIA is reported to have agreed to acquire $1 billion in newly issued NAVER shares, and the project is planned to expand, with the announced schedule extending to 2028. The main risk is material: NVIDIA’s investment is conditional on NAVER securing at least $9 billion in committed project financing, while Brookfield’s disclosed term sheet is \nv21 The market reference is roughly 87–90% FY2027 growth (Zacks estimates 87.4–88%, while the $411 billion estimate implies about 90% against FY2026 revenue), which is rung 1; the ladder's cited 42% estimate is not the current reference. Q1 and Q2 revenue plus Q3 guidance total $285.8 billion. Carrying forward the approximately 12% sequential growth implied by Q3 guidance gives Q4 revenue of about $121 billion, FY2027 revenue near $407 billion, and growth of about 88% from FY2026's $215.94 billion. The CFO's approximat\nv22 The reference is rung 2 (No): no specific market consensus or stated expectation for signing by 2027-07-01 is supplied, so the status quo is the reference. The evidence establishes that NVIDIA entered into residual-value guaranty agreements with SB Energy for leases at the Ohio site that OpenAI will lease. The phased start of obligations, with the first expected in FY2029, concerns when the guarantees take effect—not whether NVIDIA signed them. The implied outcome is therefore Yes, crossing the only rung boundary.\nv23 The market reference is rung 1: reporting says NVIDIA committed or is investing $5 billion, and NVIDIA confirmed a substantial investment. The forecast is also Yes: SSI said the investment would enable a tenfold compute expansion over the 12 months from July 27, 2026, and the companies announced a strategic collaboration. Those facts make completion by July 27, 2027 more likely than non-completion, though they do not prove that the full amount has already been transferred. The reports of an investment of $5 billion\nv24 The reference is NVIDIA's stated October 2026 shipping plan, which sits at rung 1 (Yes); no separate market consensus is supplied. The evidence implies RTX Spark systems will begin shipping in October, before the October 31 deadline: NVIDIA explicitly says shipping begins that month, while the October 16 launch and announced partner systems support the timing. The launch date itself is not proof that shipments have begun, but the forecast remains more likely Yes than No.\nv25 The required outcome is formal adoption or entry into force by 2027-01-31, not consultation or consideration. Consultations are confirmed, but the Ministry gave no timeline and the controls were only reported as being weighed. That makes adoption by the deadline less likely than no adoption; the evidence does not establish that controls will never be adopted.\nv26 The status-quo reference is rung 2, and the evidence still makes no ban by the deadline more likely than a ban. Taiwan is considering a criminal ban, and the alleged export case—along with lawmakers’ and experts’ stated view that the law needs to change—adds pressure toward legislation. But the supplied evidence shows neither a formal proposal nor a timetable; enforcement is currently proceeding under other laws. The added pressure does not make enactment by 2027-01-31 the more likely outcome.\nv27 The reference is rung 2: the investigation is formally underway and described as likely to remain an ongoing point of contention, with no Commerce deadline or consensus supplied. The access allegations and Taiwanese smuggling enforcement show the issue remains active, but they do not report a Commerce conclusion or give a timetable; they therefore do not make a public conclusion by 2027-01-31 more likely than the investigation remaining open.\nv29 The reference is Bank of America's roughly $70 billion estimate of NVIDIA's equity investments in ecosystem partners; that sits in rung 3 ($50 billion to below $75 billion), though its period and relation to outstanding commitments are unclear. The disclosed $25 billion commitment balance plus $18 billion committed for the remainder of FY2027 gives a $43 billion base. Adding the $3.5 billion MediaTek investment announced after that remainder-of-year figure, and risk-adjusting the $10 billion Anthropic and $2.5 bill\nv30 The reference is NVIDIA’s announced $1.5 billion investment plan, which sits at rung 1 (Yes). The later report that NVIDIA agreed to invest $3 billion in SB Energy supports completion of at least the planned amount, while SB Energy’s financing dependence, lack of data-center revenue, and exposure to OpenAI’s performance are meaningful execution risks. Those risks temper confidence but do not make non-completion by the deadline more likely than completion; no supplied evidence reports a cancellation or delay. The in\nv31 The reference is NVIDIA’s announced plan to invest $1.5 billion, which sits at rung 1 (Yes); no separate market consensus is supplied. The agreement, reported $3 billion investment commitment, and progress securing capacity for the Ohio project make completion by the deadline more likely than non-completion. They do not establish that the $1.5 billion has already transferred, but the binary forecast remains Yes.\nv32 The reference is the reported $6 billion licensing commitment, which is rung 3. The evidence continues to point to a $6 billion license payment; the separately reported $1 billion investment is not part of that payment, so the implied amount does not cross a rung boundary.\nv33 The reference is NVIDIA management’s FY2028 gross-margin guidance of 72%–73%, which sits at rung 3. The implied outcome remains about 72.5%, the midpoint of that direct FY2028 guidance. The guided path—75% in FY2027 Q2, 74% in Q3, and 71%–72% in Q4 before recovery—supports the forecast, while worsening memory-cost and supply signals add downside risk without quantifying a further FY2028 reduction. The implied figure remains within 71%–below 73%, so it does not cross a rung boundary.\nv35 The market reference is analyst consensus of about $678 billion for FY2028, which sits at rung 2. The current implied outcome remains around $671–$678 billion: NVIDIA's 70% outlook is about $673 billion, while the analyst forecast is $678 billion; applying Zacks' 63.2% growth estimate to the cited $411 billion FY2027 estimate gives about $671 billion. These figures remain below the $700 billion rung-1 boundary. Chip-volume and customer-demand evidence, together with favorable export-control and capex themes, suppor\nv36 The reference is NVIDIA's announced $3.5 billion convertible-bond commitment, exactly rung 3. The evidence continues to describe that same commitment and includes reports that NVIDIA bought the bonds; MediaTek's $3.9 billion total offering is not an additional NVIDIA commitment. The implied amount remains exactly $3.5 billion, so it does not cross either adjacent rung boundary.\nv37 The reference is rung 2: no company production-start guidance or market consensus is supplied, so the status-quo expectation is no start by the deadline. Kuo’s specific Q1 2027 production forecast puts the implied start within the deadline, while NVIDIA’s defined rack configuration supports product readiness. The memory redesign adds execution risk, but supplies no later date or evidence that the Q1 2027 forecast has slipped; the forecast therefore remains more likely than no start by March 31. This is a forecast, \nv38 The reference is rung 2 (No): no consensus or stated completion timetable is supplied, so the status quo is the reference. The reported fundraising is still in talks, but NVIDIA is specifically expected to supply roughly half of the $5–6 billion raise. With the report dated one year before the deadline, that makes completion by the deadline more likely than non-completion, though the evidence does not establish that the investment has occurred.\nv39 The reference is rung 2: no market consensus or DOJ completion timetable is supplied, and the review is reported to be active. The investigation and reported information request make a public conclusion by the deadline less likely than the review remaining open. The transaction’s scale and the legal and congressional scrutiny add context for a substantive review, but do not establish a conclusion date. The reported view that DOJ is unlikely to require an unwind concerns the possible remedy, not whether or when the \nv40 The reference is rung 2: no market consensus or company guidance is supplied, and the reported expectation that Anthropic's IPO would be completed before the November midterms does not put NVIDIA's investment by September 20. Reuters-linked reports that NVIDIA is considering up to $10 billion and that Anthropic is discussing an anchor role push toward Yes, but they describe consideration and discussion, not a commitment or a completed investment. With only three days to the deadline, the most likely outcome remains\nv41 The reference is the fed funds futures market's 86% probability of an increase, which sits at rung 1 (Yes). The later fact says the Federal Reserve raised interest rates on September 17, so the outcome at the meeting is Yes; this is not merely a forecast based on the reference.\nv42 The reference is the status quo—no reported faster interconnection terms or preferential tariff—which sits at rung 2. The alliance's formation, construction of the facility, and plan for shared frameworks make favorable treatment more plausible, and the article specifically raises it as a possibility. But with only a few months until the deadline, no utility agreement, tariff, or specific interconnection commitment is reported, so neither outcome remains more likely by 2026-12-31; the evidence does not cross the bo\nmargin midpoint 73.64181201550387\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import json, pathlib\ndata=json.loads(pathlib.Path('\\\\''/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad/api-library.json'\\\\'').read_text())\nvs={v['\\\\''view_id'\\\\'']:v for v in data['\\\\''views'\\\\'']}\nimport sqlite3, re\nconn=sqlite3.connect('\\\\''file:poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/items-comparison.sqlite?mode=ro'\\\\'',uri=True)\nids=dict(re.findall(r'\\\\''\"'^  - (e'\"\\\\d+) \\\\[.*? input (\\\\S+)\"'$'\"'\\\\'',pathlib.Path('\\\\''docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/council-q/library-pack.md'\\\\'').read_text(),re.M))\nes={e['\\\\''id'\\\\'']:e for v in data['\\\\''views'\\\\''] for e in v['\\\\''evidence'\\\\'']}\n\nfor k, pat in {\n'\\\\''e2497'\\\\'':r'\\\\''96\\\\.2 billion|81,615|74\\\\.9%|Data Center compute revenue|108\\\\.0 billion'\\\\'',\n'\\\\''e2574'\\\\'':r'\\\\''89\\\\.0 billion|Blackwell Ultra'\\\\'',\n'\\\\''e2269'\\\\'':r'\\\\''top four hyperscalers|\\\\\"'$760 billion'\"'\\\\'',\n'\\\\''e4696'\\\\'':r'\\\\''\\\\\"'$720 billion|'\"\\\\\"'$745 billion|87'\"\\\\.4%'\\\\'',\n'\\\\''e976'\\\\'':r'\\\\''SemiAnalysis|20% in the second half'\\\\'',\n'\\\\''e3943'\\\\'':r'\\\\''12% sequential growth'\\\\'',\n'\\\\''e4901'\\\\'':r'\\\\''\\\\\"'$100 billion|next year'\"'\\\\'',\n'\\\\''e2612'\\\\'':r'\\\\''\\\\\"'$92 billion|279 billion|material commitments'\"'\\\\'',\n}.items():\n row=conn.execute('\\\\''select title,publisher,publication_day,text from items where input_id=?'\\\\'',(ids[k],)).fetchone()\n if not row: print(k,'\\\\''MISSING'\\\\'',ids[k]);continue\n title,pub,day,t=row\n print(k,ids[k],day,'\\\\''quote literal:'\\\\'',es[k]['\\\\''quote'\\\\''] in t)\n for m in list(re.finditer(pat,t,re.I))[:7]: print(re.sub(r'\\\\''\\\\s+'\\\\'','\\\\'' '\\\\'',t[max(0,m.start()-45):m.end()+82]))\nfor v in ('\\\\''v5'\\\\'','\\\\''v6'\\\\'','\\\\''v10'\\\\'','\\\\''v41'\\\\''):\n ev=vs[v]['\\\\''evidence'\\\\''][0]\n print(v,ev['\\\\''id'\\\\''],ev['\\\\''one_line'\\\\''],ev['\\\\''quote'\\\\''],ev['\\\\''source'\\\\'']['\\\\''title'\\\\''])\n'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import json, pathlib\ndata=json.loads(pathlib.Path('\\\\''/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad/api-library.json'\\\\'').read_text())\nvs={v['\\\\''view_id'\\\\'']:v for v in data['\\\\''views'\\\\'']}\nimport sqlite3, re\nconn=sqlite3.connect('\\\\''file:poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/items-comparison.sqlite?mode=ro'\\\\'',uri=True)\nids=dict(re.findall(r'\\\\''\"'^  - (e'\"\\\\d+) \\\\[.*? input (\\\\S+)\"'$'\"'\\\\'',pathlib.Path('\\\\''docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/council-q/library-pack.md'\\\\'').read_text(),re.M))\nes={e['\\\\''id'\\\\'']:e for v in data['\\\\''views'\\\\''] for e in v['\\\\''evidence'\\\\'']}\n\nfor k, pat in {\n'\\\\''e2497'\\\\'':r'\\\\''96\\\\.2 billion|81,615|74\\\\.9%|Data Center compute revenue|108\\\\.0 billion'\\\\'',\n'\\\\''e2574'\\\\'':r'\\\\''89\\\\.0 billion|Blackwell Ultra'\\\\'',\n'\\\\''e2269'\\\\'':r'\\\\''top four hyperscalers|\\\\\"'$760 billion'\"'\\\\'',\n'\\\\''e4696'\\\\'':r'\\\\''\\\\\"'$720 billion|'\"\\\\\"'$745 billion|87'\"\\\\.4%'\\\\'',\n'\\\\''e976'\\\\'':r'\\\\''SemiAnalysis|20% in the second half'\\\\'',\n'\\\\''e3943'\\\\'':r'\\\\''12% sequential growth'\\\\'',\n'\\\\''e4901'\\\\'':r'\\\\''\\\\\"'$100 billion|next year'\"'\\\\'',\n'\\\\''e2612'\\\\'':r'\\\\''\\\\\"'$92 billion|279 billion|material commitments'\"'\\\\'',\n}.items():\n row=conn.execute('\\\\''select title,publisher,publication_day,text from items where input_id=?'\\\\'',(ids[k],)).fetchone()\n if not row: print(k,'\\\\''MISSING'\\\\'',ids[k]);continue\n title,pub,day,t=row\n print(k,ids[k],day,'\\\\''quote literal:'\\\\'',es[k]['\\\\''quote'\\\\''] in t)\n for m in list(re.finditer(pat,t,re.I))[:7]: print(re.sub(r'\\\\''\\\\s+'\\\\'','\\\\'' '\\\\'',t[max(0,m.start()-45):m.end()+82]))\nfor v in ('\\\\''v5'\\\\'','\\\\''v6'\\\\'','\\\\''v10'\\\\'','\\\\''v41'\\\\''):\n ev=vs[v]['\\\\''evidence'\\\\''][0]\n print(v,ev['\\\\''id'\\\\''],ev['\\\\''one_line'\\\\''],ev['\\\\''quote'\\\\''],ev['\\\\''source'\\\\'']['\\\\''title'\\\\''])\n'\"","aggregated_output":"e2497 nvidianews:nvidia-announces-financial-results-for-second-quarter-fiscal-2027 2026-08-26 quote literal: True\ns for Second Quarter Fiscal 2027 Revenue of $96.2 billion, up 106% from a year ago Data Center revenue of $89.0 billion, up 117% from a yea\n the second quarter ended July 26, 2026, of $96.2 billion, up 18% from the previous quarter and up 106% from a year ago. For the quarter, G\n27 is as follows: Revenue is expected to be $108.0 billion, plus or minus 2%. NVIDIA is not assuming any Data Center compute revenue from Ch\nplus or minus 2%. NVIDIA is not assuming any Data Center compute revenue from China in its outlook. GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins are expected to be 74.\ne2574 edgar:NVDA:0001045810-26-000073:q2fy27cfocommentary.htm 2026-08-26 quote literal: True\nrevenue for the second quarter was a record $89.0 billion, up 117% from a year ago and up 18% sequentially, driven by the ramp of our Black\n 18% sequentially, driven by the ramp of our Blackwell Ultra infrastructure. Hyperscale revenue more than doubled from a year ago and increase\nncreased 13% sequentially on the strength of Blackwell Ultra. ACIE revenue increased 138% from a year ago and 25% sequentially driven by end-d\ncreased from a year ago on improved mix from Blackwell Ultra. GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins were approximately flat sequentially as our Blac\ne2269 gnews:c8ea23246edff9fbbc981992 2026-08-23 quote literal: True\n sustainability of the AI building boom. The top four hyperscalers plan $760 billion in capex in 2026 alone. That level of spending leaves investors\nuilding boom. The top four hyperscalers plan $760 billion in capex in 2026 alone. That level of spending leaves investors wondering how muc\ne4696 gnews:1d3bc1ba4c2df5e998884bdb 2026-09-14 quote literal: True\ntforms — alone are expected to spend roughly $720 billion to $745 billion on capital expenditures in 2026, with a large portion directed to\nre expected to spend roughly $720 billion to $745 billion on capital expenditures in 2026, with a large portion directed toward AI infrastr\n and EPS indicates a year-over-year surge of 87.4% and 93.5%, respectively. The consensus mark for fiscal 2028 revenues and EPS sign\ne976 gnews:bdd09874d53aef7b27866d44 2026-07-26 quote literal: True\nlier this month, semiconductor research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia ( NVDA -4.40% ) was facing major setbacks with its Kyber NVL\ndelayed over 12 months to 2028, according to SemiAnalysis. Nvidia issued a brief statement saying its roadmap is intact. More recently, CEO\n on Huang's recent comments. And even though SemiAnalysis reported the delays, it's still bullish on Nvidia, forecasting that the chipmaker\nter revenue will exceed analyst estimates by 20% in the second half of its fiscal 2027. The concrete data will be in Nvidia's upcoming earnings calls\ne3943 gnews:89698c9955cf8aa35b642970 2026-09-04 quote literal: True\ns fiscal 2027 third-quarter guidance implies 12% sequential growth at the midpoint. Strong financial results, recent growth acceleration, and the Ve\ne4901 gnews:3a284e233d8b31c62dc66b33 2026-09-18 quote literal: True\nfall short of what customers need by roughly $100 billion next year. It’s a situation in which the company is playing a growing role as it \n what customers need by roughly $100 billion next year. It’s a situation in which the company is playing a growing role as it increasing\nlette Kress said annual revenue could double next year if it wasn’t for significant supply constraints felt by its suppliers. “Our entir\ny disclosing its revenue growth forecast for next year: a whopping 70 percent. This raises the prospect that Nvidia—which financial anal\ncompany’s claim that it could double revenue next year if it wasn’t constrained by the global production capacity of its suppliers. “Inc\nmers’ forecasts point to our growth doubling next year. However, as I mentioned earlier, we expect to grow approximately 70 percent as w\ncould leave demand unfulfilled for more than $100 billion in AI infrastructure gear, which includes its GPUs as well as other kinds of chip\ne2612 edgar:NVDA:0001045810-26-000075:nvda-20260726.htm 2026-08-26 quote literal: True\nitments from $ 119 billion last quarter to $ 279 billion as of July 26, 2026. These supply commitments are for our data center infrastruct\nmmitments from $119 billion last quarter to $279 billion as of July 26, 2026 to meet future demand. Refer to Note 10 of the Notes to Conde\nv5 e34 The four largest AI hyperscalers plan combined data-center capital expenditures of around $650 billion in calendar 2026. The big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year. Nvidia Stock Is Nearly Flat for 2026. Time to Cash Out, or Load Up on Shares?\nv6 e35 NVIDIA projects that the four largest AI hyperscalers’ combined data-center capital expenditures could reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027. Next year, Nvidia projects that this figure could reach $1 trillion. Nvidia Stock Is Nearly Flat for 2026. Time to Cash Out, or Load Up on Shares?\nv10 e135 Chinese officials reportedly informed Alibaba, ByteDance and DeepSeek that they would be permitted to buy some Nvidia H200 chips; the purchases remain subject to approval. Chinese officials have informed companies such as Alibaba Group, ByteDance and DeepSeek that they will have permission to purchase some of the processors, which are used to develop AI models, the news site said on July 8 . China to let AI firms buy Nvidia H200s, says report\nv41 e4684 Fed funds futures markets priced about an 86% chance of a rate increase at the Federal Reserve’s September 2026 policy meeting. The Federal Reserve opens its September policy meeting this week, with fed funds futures markets assigning about an 86% chance to a rate increase. Nvidia, chip stocks fall as AI CEOs call for slowdown\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
