output":"e34 gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568 2026-07-04  its clients everything they need to run an Nvidia-powered data center, locking in clients once they've chosen to use its products. This bodes well for the future, as AI hyperscalers are spending a massive amount of money this year and beyond to continue building out their AI computing footprints. The big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year. Next year, Nvidia projects that this figure could reach $1 trillion. That means more growth is coming to Nvidia, and the investing story is far from complete. Wall Street analysts project 82% revenue growth for this year and 41% next year, so there's another external source that's projecting even more growth for Nvidia. However, the stock price doesn't reflect that. Nvidia trades at 21.7 times forward earnings, essentially the same price\ne35 gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568 2026-07-04  its clients everything they need to run an Nvidia-powered data center, locking in clients once they've chosen to use its products. This bodes well for the future, as AI hyperscalers are spending a massive amount of money this year and beyond to continue building out their AI computing footprints. The big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year. Next year, Nvidia projects that this figure could reach $1 trillion. That means more growth is coming to Nvidia, and the investing story is far from complete. Wall Street analysts project 82% revenue growth for this year and 41% next year, so there's another external source that's projecting even more growth for Nvidia. However, the stock price doesn't reflect that. Nvidia trades at 21.7 times forward earnings, essentially the same price\ne135 gnews:c8c39f971721fd820aab5c1c 2026-07-08 igh AI demand but aims to protect China's chip industry and address cybersecurity concerns amid US-China tech tensions. China plans to allow top artificial intelligence companies to buy a limited amount of H200 chips from Nvidia, a sign the country is easing restrictions on the coveted US technology, according to the Information news site. Chinese officials have informed companies such as Alibaba Group, ByteDance and DeepSeek that they will have permission to purchase some of the processors, which are used to develop AI models, the news site said on July 8 . The companies need to say how many chips they need – and why – to get approval, according to the Information. The chips have emerged as a geopolitical flashpoint for the world’s two largest economies. Though US President Donald Trump granted Nvidia permission to sell the processors to\nv28 2026-08-06 3 The reference is NVIDIA’s FY2027 “mid-seventies” gross-margin guidance, which sits at rung 3. The only in-force evidence is that guidance itself; it implies a mid-seventies outcome and provides no independent quantified change to move the forecast across a rung boundary.\nv28 2026-08-26 3 The reference is NVIDIA’s FY2027 “mid-seventies” guidance, which sits at rung 3. The new quarterly profile lowers the implied full-year result from a roughly 75% run-rate: Q1 and Q2 were 75%, Q3 is guided to 74%, and Q4 to 71–72%. That still implies about 73.8% on an equal-quarter average, within rung 3 and above its 73% floor. Memory-cost pressure and the guided Q4 trough weigh against a higher rung; reported early-2027 system price increases provide some offset but do not quantify a gross-margin improvement large enough to change th\nv28 2026-09-03 3 The reference is NVIDIA's FY2027 “mid-seventies” gross-margin guidance, which sits at rung 3. The quarterly path now implies about 73.7% for the year: that remains above rung 3's 73% floor and below rung 2's 76% threshold. The Q4 trough and memory-cost pressure keep the result from reaching rung 2; the announced price increases support recovery, but take effect in FY2028 Q1, after this forecast period.\nv28 2026-09-19 3 The reference is NVIDIA's FY2027 “mid-seventies” gross-margin guidance, which sits at rung 3. The quarterly path implies about 73.6%–73.7% for FY2027, below the reference's midpoint but still at least 73%; it does not cross into rung 4. Memory-cost pressure and the likely dilutive H200 sales are downside risks, while price increases and potentially favorable HBM terms offer offsets. The updated China-authorization theme adds some downside exposure, but provides no volume estimate that would justify overriding the guided quarterly path\nv34 2026-08-26 1 The reference is rung 2: no market consensus or official signing deadline is supplied, and the latest explicit status says no signed agreement yet. The evidence nevertheless makes signing by the due date more likely than not: The Information reported an agreement at $12.9 billion, while the counter-report describes advanced talks that could still fall apart, not a termination. With about six months remaining, the reported agreement in principle outweighs the risk that talks fail, so the forecast crosses to rung 1.\nv34 2026-08-26 1 The reference is rung 2: no market consensus or official signing deadline is supplied, and the latest explicit status says no signed agreement yet. The evidence nevertheless makes signing by the due date more likely than not: The Information reported an agreement at $12.9 billion, while the counter-report describes advanced talks that could still fall apart, not a termination. With about six months remaining, the reported agreement in principle outweighs the risk that talks fail, so the forecast crosses to rung 1.\nv34 2026-09-03 1 The reference is rung 2 (No): no market consensus, company guidance, or stated signing deadline is supplied, so the status quo is no agreement by the due date. The SEC filing states NVIDIA entered a definitive agreement on September 2, 2026, well before February 26, 2027; that directly establishes the rung-1 outcome and crosses the boundary. Earlier reports that talks had not yet produced a signed agreement describe the status before that signing and do not outweigh the filing.\nv34 2026-09-19 1 The reference is rung 2 (No): no separate market consensus or signing deadline is supplied, so the status quo is no signed agreement. The SEC filing says NVIDIA entered into a definitive agreement on September 2, 2026, which establishes a signature before the due date and crosses to rung 1. The August 31 report that no final agreement had been signed predates that agreement; the later unsized exploration report does not outweigh the filing. The expected first-half-2027 closing date concerns completion, not whether an agreement was sig\nv4 2026-07-02 1 The reference is not independently stated: no market consensus, company guidance, or formal shipment deadline is supplied. The binary question's threshold is 2026-12-31 (rung 1 if shipping has begun by then). The available report says Vera Rubin is in full production and could begin shipping later in 2026, making a 2026 start the more likely forecast, though the conditional wording leaves meaningful uncertainty; it does not establish that shipments have already begun.\nv4 2026-08-26 1 The reference is NVIDIA’s stated production-shipment timing of fiscal Q3 2027, which falls before the 2026-12-31 deadline and therefore sits at rung 1. More decisively, NVIDIA’s CFO said production shipments commenced in August 2026; that confirms the outcome, so the unspecific delay reports do not change the rung.\nv4 2026-09-03 1 The reference is NVIDIA management's plan for production shipments to begin in fiscal Q3 2027, which is before the 2026-12-31 deadline and therefore sits at rung 1. The evidence also reports that production shipments actually began in August 2026. Earlier reports of delays had no revised schedule and do not outweigh that direct report of shipments already underway. The previous conclusion was also rung 1 on the same key fact; the additional reports shown do not change the supporting outcome.\nv4 2026-09-19 1 The reference is NVIDIA's schedule for Vera Rubin production shipments to begin in FY2027 Q3, which is before the 2026-12-31 deadline and sits at rung 1. NVIDIA said shipments began in August 2026, and CoreWeave reported seven NVL72 racks totaling 504 GPUs in production, so the evidence establishes that shipping had begun before the deadline.\nv9 2026-07-06 2 The evidence implies about $410 billion, within rung 2 ($400 billion to below $450 billion). This is a forecast, not an established FY2027 result: it carries the observed 21% sequential growth from Q1 through the remaining three quarters. The resulting estimate is above $400 billion but does not cross the $450 billion boundary. No consensus or company FY2027 guidance is supplied, so the market reference cannot be located on the ladder.\nv9 2026-08-26 2 The carried market reference is approximately $372B, which sits at rung 3. The evidence now implies about $419B, in rung 2: the reported Q2 result and Q3 outlook raise the run-rate, and carrying that growth forward with SemiAnalysis’s H2 outperformance forecast puts the result above $400B but below $450B. The forward estimate remains a forecast, not an established FY2027 result.\nv9 2026-09-03 2 The market reference carried from the previous judgement is approximately $372 billion, which sits in rung 3. The evidence-implied estimate remains about $422 billion: FY2027 Q1 and Q2 contribute $164.25 billion; the $108 billion Q3 total-revenue outlook, applied to the latest reported 92.5% Data Center mix, implies about $99.9 billion; and adding the carried Q4 baseline of about $115 billion gives an H2 baseline near $215 billion. Applying SemiAnalysis's forecast that H2 Data Center revenue will exceed estimates by 20% implies roughl\nv9 2026-09-19 3 The reference is approximately $414 billion for FY2027, which sits at rung 2. The current evidence implies about $385–390 billion: the first two quarters produced about $164 billion, Q3 Data Center revenue is approximately $100 billion using the $108 billion total-revenue guide and recent Data Center mix, and Q4 is estimated at roughly $120–125 billion as hyperscale growth reaccelerates and Rubin supply builds. That remains below the $400 billion rung-2 boundary. Strong demand and Rubin orders lift the Q4 estimate modestly, but the 20\nest. 113.57495999999999 127.2039552 418.5789152 1.18625\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
