{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import json, pathlib\ndata=json.loads(pathlib.Path('\\\\''/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-lchingen-hivemind/f1ea972d-85a1-4f27-8010-cf0b60054109/scratchpad/api-library.json'\\\\'').read_text())\nvs={v['\\\\''view_id'\\\\'']:v for v in data['\\\\''views'\\\\'']}\nimport sqlite3, re\nconn=sqlite3.connect('\\\\''file:poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/items-comparison.sqlite?mode=ro'\\\\'',uri=True)\nids=dict(re.findall(r'\\\\''\"'^  - (e'\"\\\\d+) \\\\[.*? input (\\\\S+)\"'$'\"'\\\\'',pathlib.Path('\\\\''docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/council-q/library-pack.md'\\\\'').read_text(),re.M))\nes={e['\\\\''id'\\\\'']:e for v in data['\\\\''views'\\\\''] for e in v['\\\\''evidence'\\\\'']}\n\nfor k in ('\\\\''e3645'\\\\'','\\\\''e4875'\\\\'','\\\\''e5088'\\\\'','\\\\''e3768'\\\\'','\\\\''e5086'\\\\''):\n title,day,t=conn.execute('\\\\''select title,publication_day,text from items where input_id=?'\\\\'',(ids[k],)).fetchone()\n print(k,ids[k],day)\n pat={'\\\\''e3645'\\\\'':r'\\\\''Texas|freeze|grid'\\\\'','\\\\''e4875'\\\\'':r'\\\\''ByteDance|exceptions'\\\\'','\\\\''e5088'\\\\'':r'\\\\''regulatory front'\\\\'','\\\\''e3768'\\\\'':r'\\\\''Item 8.01'\\\\'','\\\\''e5086'\\\\'':r'\\\\''CEO Jensen Huang disclosed'\\\\''}.get(k)\n for m in list(re.finditer(pat,t,re.I))[:2]: print(re.sub(r'\\\\''\\\\s+'\\\\'','\\\\'' '\\\\'',t[max(0,m.start()-40):m.end()+750]))\nprint('\\\\''q1 sources'\\\\'',conn.execute(\\\"select input_id,title from items where title like '\\\\''%announces financial results for first quarter fiscal 2027%'\\\\'' limit 10\\\").fetchall())\nfor e in vs['\\\\''v9'\\\\'']['\\\\''evidence'\\\\'']:\n if re.search(r'\\\\''\\\\\"'$414'\"\\\\b'\\\\'',e['\\\\''one_line'\\\\'']):\n  print('\\\\''414'\\\\'',e['\\\\''id'\\\\''],e['\\\\''one_line'\\\\''],e['\\\\''source'\\\\'']['\\\\''title'\\\\''])\n'\"","aggregated_output":"e3645 gnews:b7a72f34e62112b669d71e36 2026-09-01\ntely 1.4% to $217.76 Tuesday morning as Texas started separating real data-center projects from grid paperwork. The stakes are enormous. Electricity requests across parts of the United States now exceed 700 gigawattsmore than ten times estimated current data-center consumption. Texas has frozen new grid connections while officials audit developers. Utilities in other markets are demanding cash upfront or proof that proposed sites are actually controlled. Nvidia's near-term machine, however, is still roaring. Its second-quarter results delivered $96.2 billion in revenue, including a staggering $89 billion from data centers. Management then guided the following quarter to approximately $108 billion. Here is the pressure point: Nvidia is selling chips today, but the 700-gigawatt pipeline\nparating real data-center projects from grid paperwork. The stakes are enormous. Electricity requests across parts of the United States now exceed 700 gigawattsmore than ten times estimated current data-center consumption. Texas has frozen new grid connections while officials audit developers. Utilities in other markets are demanding cash upfront or proof that proposed sites are actually controlled. Nvidia's near-term machine, however, is still roaring. Its second-quarter results delivered $96.2 billion in revenue, including a staggering $89 billion from data centers. Management then guided the following quarter to approximately $108 billion. Here is the pressure point: Nvidia is selling chips today, but the 700-gigawatt pipeline represents tomorrow's expectations. Removing fantasy p\ne4875 gnews:75cbbaecc2d997d4fa762033 2026-09-17\nk-market imports, and 2026 saw official exceptions issued to ByteDance, Alibaba, and Tencent . The first way to get a 'forbidden' chip into China via quasi-legal means is by simply getting a Chinese university or research institution to buy it. These entities reportedly include Nvidia GPUs inside \"sprawling multi-vendor contracts,\" routed through small Chinese regional integrators. The report also claims that some buyer institutions have ties to the CCP and the country's defense and intelligence sectors. C4ADS says that it tracked 56 chips worth $1.7 million sold this way in the report's July 2025 to January 2026 period. Additionally, it says that its 2024 investigation covering multiple years of government records revealed $6.48 million worth of silicon heading to China in this manner. Th\n 2026 saw official exceptions issued to ByteDance, Alibaba, and Tencent . The first way to get a 'forbidden' chip into China via quasi-legal means is by simply getting a Chinese university or research institution to buy it. These entities reportedly include Nvidia GPUs inside \"sprawling multi-vendor contracts,\" routed through small Chinese regional integrators. The report also claims that some buyer institutions have ties to the CCP and the country's defense and intelligence sectors. C4ADS says that it tracked 56 chips worth $1.7 million sold this way in the report's July 2025 to January 2026 period. Additionally, it says that its 2024 investigation covering multiple years of government records revealed $6.48 million worth of silicon heading to China in this manner. The second route for s\ne5088 gnews:7a5002df93385ecef12a2cd8 2026-09-20\nelf could shake that foundation. On the regulatory front, the transaction meets the mandatory filing threshold under the Hart-Scott-Rodino Antitrust Improvements Act, requiring notification to the U.S. Federal Trade Commission (FTC) and the Department of Justice. Deal completion is targeted for the first half of 2027, but approvals from regulators in multiple jurisdictions, including the EU, are also required. UK technology publication The Register published an article titled \"Hugging Face is too important to fall into NVIDIA's hands,\" arguing that regulators should intervene. Competitors are likely to file objections with antitrust authorities, and the U.S. Department of Justice has previously conducted antitrust investigations into NVIDIA following competitor complaints. In a CNBC interview, H\ne3768 edgar:NVDA:0001045810-26-000078:nvda-20260902.htm 2026-09-03\niest event reported): September 2, 2026 Item 8.01. Other Events. On September 2, 2026, NVIDIA Corporation (“NVIDIA”) entered into a definitive agreement to acquire Hugging Face, Inc. (“Hugging Face”). Hugging Face operates a platform and community for developing, sharing and deploying open-source models, datasets and applications. The transaction includes an approximately $11.9 billion purchase price payable to Hugging Face stockholders, subject to certain adjustments, and an equity-based retention program of up to approximately $1.0 billion for Hugging Face employees joining NVIDIA. The transaction is expected to close in the first half of 2027, subject to the satisfaction or waiver of customary closing conditions, including receipt of required regulatory approvals. NVIDIA expects the ac\ne5086 gnews:7a5002df93385ecef12a2cd8 2026-09-20\nnt was signed on September 2, 2026, and CEO Jensen Huang disclosed the deal in an official blog post on September 3. In terms of cash outlay, this ranks as NVIDIA's second-largest transaction in company history and its largest outright acquisition. Notably, it was not NVIDIA that initiated the acquisition. Hugging Face CEO Clément Delangue personally visited Huang in summer 2026 to propose a collaboration aimed at expanding open-source AI. The company had previously rejected a roughly $500 million (approximately ¥78 billion) investment from NVIDIA in 2025. The shift from defending independence to voluntarily seeking shelter reflects structural changes in the AI industry and a sense of urgency about maintaining momentum for open models. From Investment Rejection to Acquisition Agreement Hugging Face is a p\nq1 sources []\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
